The '''2026 New Zealand general election''' is scheduled to be held on 7 November 2026.<ref>{{cite news |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/584605/watch-live-prime-minister-christopher-luxon-announces-election-day-2026-date |title=As it happened: Election to be held on 7 November, Christopher Luxon announces |date=21 January 2026 |access-date=21 January 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-01-21 |title=Christopher Luxon announces election date for 7 November |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/584623/christopher-luxon-announces-election-date-for-7-november |access-date=2026-01-21 |website=RNZ |language=en-nz}}</ref> Voters will elect 120 members to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives|House of Representatives]] under New Zealand's [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) voting system, comprising 71 members elected from single-member [[New Zealand electorates|electorates]] and 49 members elected from [[closed list|closed]] [[party list]]s to achieve [[proportional representation|proportionality]].

After the previous election, the centre-right [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]], led by prime minister [[Christopher Luxon]], formed [[Sixth National Government of New Zealand|a coalition government]] with the [[ACT New Zealand|ACT]] and [[New Zealand First]] parties. The [[Official Opposition (New Zealand)|official Opposition]] to the government is the centre-left [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]], led by former prime minister [[Chris Hipkins]]. Other opposition parties are the left-wing [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] and the indigenous rights-based [[Te Pāti Māori]].

== Electoral system ==
{{main|Electoral system of New Zealand}}

New Zealand uses the [[mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) voting system to elect the House of Representatives. Each voter gets two votes, one for a political party (the party vote) and one for a local candidate (the electorate vote). Political parties which meet the threshold (5% of the party vote or one electorate seat) receive seats in the House in proportion to the percentage of the [[party vote]] they receive. At this election, 71 of the 120 seats will be filled by the MPs elected from the [[New Zealand electorates|electorates]], with the winner in each electorate determined by the [[first-past-the-post voting|first past the post method]] (i.e. most votes wins). The remaining 49 seats will be filled by candidates from each party's closed [[party list]]. If a party wins more electorates than seats it is entitled to under the party vote, an [[overhang seat|overhang]] results; in this case, the House will add extra seats to cover the overhang.

The political party or party bloc with the majority of the seats in the House forms the [[Government of New Zealand|Government]]. Since the introduction of MMP in 1996, only once has a party won an outright majority of seats, when the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] won 65 out of 120 seats in [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]]. Usually, parties negotiate with each other to form a [[coalition government]] or a [[minority government]].

===Electorate boundaries===
Electorate boundaries in New Zealand are redrawn after every census. The number of [[South Island]] general electorates is fixed at 16,<ref>Electoral Act 1993, section 35(3)(a).</ref> with the number of [[North Island]] general electorates and Māori electorates increasing or decreasing in proportion.  For the 2020 and 2023 elections, there were 49 North Island general electorates and seven Māori electorates, leaving 48 seats to be elected from party lists.<ref>{{cite web|title=How are electoral boundaries decided?|url=https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/what-are-electorates/how-are-electoral-boundaries-decided/|publisher=Electoral Commission|accessdate=7 February 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=New Zealand Election Results |url=https://www.justice.govt.nz/justice-sector-policy/key-initiatives/maori-electoral-option |accessdate=7 February 2024 |publisher=Ministry of Justice}}</ref>

On 25 October 2024, [[Statistics New Zealand]] announced that, following the [[2023 New Zealand census|2023 census]], the number of North Island general electorates would decrease from 49 to 48, with the number of list seats increasing from 48 to 49. Nearly half of North Island electorates were outside the permitted ±5% population tolerance, necessitating extensive boundary changes across [[Auckland]], [[Wellington]], and the [[Bay of Plenty]].<ref>{{cite web |date=23 October 2024 |title=General electorates down by one, number of Māori electorates stays at seven |url=https://www.stats.govt.nz/news/general-electorates-down-by-one-number-of-maori-electorates-stays-at-seven/ |work=Stats NZ}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Number of electorates and electoral populations: 2023 Census {{!}} Stats NZ |url=https://www.stats.govt.nz/information-releases/number-of-electorates-and-electoral-populations-2023-census |access-date=9 November 2024 |website=www.stats.govt.nz}}</ref> The Representation Commission released draft boundaries for public consultation on 25 March 2025,<ref>{{cite web |date=25 March 2025 |title=Proposed electorate boundaries released |url=https://elections.nz/media-and-news/2025/media-release-proposed-electorate-boundaries-released// |work=Elections NZ}}</ref> and finalised boundaries on 8 August 2025.<ref name=":0" />

In western Auckland, [[New Lynn (New Zealand electorate)|New Lynn]], [[Kelston (New Zealand electorate)|Kelston]] and [[Te Atatū (New Zealand electorate)|Te Atatū]] were reconfigured into [[Waitākere (New Zealand electorate)|Waitākere]], {{NZ electorate link|Glendene}} and {{NZ electorate link|Henderson}}. In southern Auckland, the electorate of [[Panmure-Ōtāhuhu]] became [[Ōtāhuhu (New Zealand electorate)|Ōtāhuhu]] after losing the Panmure area. In Bay of Plenty, [[Tauranga (New Zealand electorate)|Tauranga]] and [[Bay of Plenty (New Zealand electorate)|Bay of Plenty]] were substantially reconfigured, with Bay of Plenty renamed {{NZ electorate link|Mount Maunganui}}. On the East Coast, [[East Coast (New Zealand electorate)|East Coast]] was renamed [[East Cape (New Zealand electorate)|East Cape]]. In the lower [[North Island]], Ōtaki, Mana and Ōhāriu were reconfigured into the {{NZ electorate link|Kenepuru}} and [[Kapiti (New Zealand electorate)|Kapiti]] electorates. In Wellington, [[Wellington Central (New Zealand electorate)|Wellington Central]] and [[Rongotai (New Zealand electorate)|Rongotai]] were renamed {{NZ electorate link|Wellington North}} and {{NZ electorate link|Wellington Bays}} respectively following substantial boundary changes.<ref name=":0">{{cite web |date=8 August 2025 |title=Electorate boundaries finalised |url=https://elections.nz/media-and-news/2025/electorate-boundaries-finalised/// |work=Elections NZ}}</ref>

===Changes to electoral system===
On 16 December 2025, the New Zealand Parliament passed the [[Electoral Amendment Act 2025]], which amended the [[Electoral Act 1993]] ahead of the 2026 general election. The new law limits voter enrolment to 13 days before polling day, [[Voting rights of prisoners in New Zealand|banned prisoners from voting]], raised the threshold for voluntary donations from NZ$5,000 to NZ$6,000 and banned the distribution of food and most drinks near polling booths.<ref name="Electoral Amendment Act 2025">{{cite act |type=Act of Parliament |date=19 December 2025|article=Government Bill 186-3|legislature=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |title=Electoral Amendment Bill|url=https://legislation.govt.nz/bill/government/2025/0186/latest/LMS1459916.html}}</ref><ref name="Palmer 17 Dec 2025">{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=Electoral Amendment Bill passes its third reading in Parliament |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/582069/electoral-amendment-bill-passes-its-third-reading-in-parliament |access-date=18 December 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=17 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251217032844/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/582069/electoral-amendment-bill-passes-its-third-reading-in-parliament |archive-date=17 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> The law change was supported by the governing [[New Zealand National Party|National]], [[ACT New Zealand|ACT]] and [[New Zealand First]] parties but was opposed by the opposition [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]], [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green]] parties, and [[Te Pāti Māori]], who described it as amounting to [[voter suppression]] and discriminatory against younger and ethnic minority voters.<ref name="Palmer 17 Dec 2025" /><ref name="Select committee report">{{cite web |title=Final report (Electoral Amendment Bill and Report of the Attorney-General under NZBORA on the bill) |url=https://selectcommittees.parliament.nz/view/SelectCommitteeReport/d6b08626-21ad-4ac9-90a8-08de2d34dcb8 |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=2 March 2026 |date=27 November 2025}}</ref>

On 15 July 2026, the Electoral Commission announced that it would default to sending electoral information (including the EasyVote cards and information about candidates) to voters via email instead of mail. From 2026, voters will only receive their information in the post if the Electoral Commission does not have their email addresses on their file. Chief Electoral Officer Karl Le Quesne attributed the switch from post to email to the shrinkage of [[New Zealand Post]]'s services. Te Hapori Matihiko founder and chief executive Katie Brown expressed concerns that this digital switch could create barriers for many people.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dirga |first1=Nik |title=Explainer: Why EasyVote cards may no longer be mailed to you |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/723247/explainer-why-easyvote-cards-may-no-longer-be-mailed-to-you |access-date=16 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=15 July 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260715111307/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/723247/explainer-why-easyvote-cards-may-no-longer-be-mailed-to-you |archive-date=15 July 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Voter enrolment===
In early July 2026, the [[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] confirmed that more than half of [[Māori people|ethnic Māori]] voters (302,096) were on the [[Māori electorates|Māori roll]] while 255,627 were registered on the general roll. Enrolled Māori voters were able to switch between the general and Māori electoral rolls up to 6 August 2026.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Molyneux |first1=Taiha |title=More than half of Māori voters now on Māori electoral roll |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/te-ao-maori/680344/more-than-half-of-maori-voters-now-on-maori-electoral-roll |access-date=21 August 2026 |work=6 July 2026 |date=6 July 2026}}</ref> By mid-August, [[Radio New Zealand]] reported that overseas enrolments across the [[Māori electorates]] had increased by 67% since the [[2023 New Zealand general election|previous general election]]. Queensland-based enrolment advocates Rachael Marie Johnstone and Nicole Orzecki attributed this surge to political upheaval in New Zealand boosting voter enrolment among [[Māori Australians]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Molyneux |first1=Taiha |title=Political upheaval drives huge surge in Māori overseas enrolment |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/te-ao-maori/1058552/political-upheaval-drives-huge-surge-in-maori-overseas-enrolment |access-date=21 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=19 August 2026}}</ref>

In early August 2026, [[Radio New Zealand]] reported that youth voter enrolment has plummeted to 53% by 30 June 2026. This was the lowest figure in a 15-year decline in voter enrolment between 2011 and 2026. On 30 June 2011, 70% of young people between the ages of 18 and 24 years had enrolled to vote. By 2023, this figure had declined to 60% of young people. [[Auckland University of Technology]] [[political scientist]] Julianne Molineaux said that compulsory civics education in schools and lowering the voting age to 16 years would help combat the decline in youth voter engagement. Electoral Commission strategic engagement and partnerships director Shane Whitfield said that the Commission would seek to mobilise eligible youth voters via social media, radio and television broadcasting campaigns, sports clubs, schools, universities, polytechnics and malls.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Crimp |first1=Lauren |title='Things are screwed anyway, why try?' Youth voter enrolment rate plummets |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/868515/things-are-screwed-anyway-why-try-youth-voter-enrolment-rate-plummets |access-date=3 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=3 August 2026}}</ref> Make It 16 national secretary Malak Tamimi attributed the low youth voter enrolment to young people not seeing themselves being heard by politicians and other decision-makers while the group's national director Jamie Fountain urged the Government to lower the voting age to 16 years to raise youth engagement.<ref name="Bailey-McDowell and Lance 21 Oct 2026">{{cite news |last1=Bailey-McDowell |first1=Layla |last2=Lance |first2=Coco |title=Youth aren't disengaged, they're unheard – rangatahi |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2044679/youth-aren-t-disengaged-they-re-unheard-rangatahi |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=21 October 2026}}</ref>

On 10 August, Chief Electoral Officer Karl Le Quesne acknowledged that 115,000 people were currently listed on the dormant roll, about 25% lower than the same time during the leadup to the [[2023 New Zealand general election]]. Voters are placed on the dormant roll if the Electoral Commission is unable to contact them. While dormant voters are able to cast a vote, their votes will be counted as [[special vote]]s and they would not be sent EasyVote cards.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Labour MP sounds alarm about 115,000 on dormant electoral roll |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/956884/labour-mp-sounds-alarm-about-115-000-on-dormant-electoral-roll |access-date=12 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=11 August 2026}}</ref>

By 15 October, the Electoral Commission reported that 65.5% of 18–24 year olds had enrolled to vote.<ref name="Hanly 18 Oct 2026">{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Opposition parties raise concerns over what they say is voter suppression |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1987931/opposition-parties-raise-concerns-over-what-they-say-is-voter-suppression |access-date=19 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=18 October 2026}}</ref><ref name="RNZ 19 Oct 2026">{{cite news |title=National leader Christopher Luxon laughs off opposition's charge of voter suppression |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2014525/national-leader-christopher-luxon-laughs-off-opposition-s-charge-of-voter-suppression |access-date=19 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=19 October 2026}}</ref> By comparison, 76.7% percent of 18–24 year old were enrolled during the [[2023 New Zealand general election]]. Le Quesne said the Commission had promoted voter enrollment by attending 2,700 events and venues, collecting over 28,000 enrolment forms and contracting 36 community groups to mobilise voters. As of mid October 2026, the Commission's voter enrolment and information campaigns had amounted to NZ$13 million.<ref name="RNZ 19 Oct 2026" /> In addition, several youth groups and individuals including Make It 16, Tomorrow Together, Future Generations the [[Otago University Students' Association]],  and Platinum launched campaigns and events to boost youth voter enrolment in order to get decision makers to address issues of interests to youth including cost of living pressures, education, climate change, and homelessness.<ref name="Bailey-McDowell and Lance 21 Oct 2026" /><ref>{{cite news |last1=Shaw |first1=Ruby |title='Show up or shut up': Young voters urged to enrol, have their say |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/news/dunedin/show-up-or-shut-up-young-voters-urged-to-enrol-have-their-say-of0h3n9j |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |date=21 October 2026|url-access=subscription}}</ref><ref name="Bailey-McDowell 12 Oct 2026">{{cite news |last1=Bailey-McDowell |first1=Layla |title=Youth advocates urge rangatahi to vote as homelessness concerns grow |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1860476/youth-advocates-urge-rangatahi-to-vote-as-homelessness-concerns-grow |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=12 October 2026}}</ref>

On 18 October, [[Radio New Zealand]] reported that Prime Minister Luxon had picked 8 November as the election date despite the Electoral Commission favouring 17 October and 21 November as alternative dates. The Electoral Commission had regarded 8 November as "challenging, but manageable" due to advance voting commencing on [[Labour Day]] on 26 October (which is a [[public holiday]]).<ref name="Hanly election date">{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Prime Minister passed over easier election dates, advice reveals |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1974290/prime-minister-passed-over-easier-election-dates-advice-reveals |access-date=19 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=18 October 2026}}</ref> In response, several opposition figures including Labour Party leader Hipkins, Greens co-leader Swarbrick, and Labour MP [[Camilla Belich]] described the Government's abolition of same-day enrolments and choice of election date as amounting to [[voter suppression]] aimed at discouraged left-leaning voters from participating.<ref name="Hanly 18 Oct 2026" /><ref name="Hanly election date" /> A National Party spokesperson countered that the election date had been public knowledge for nine months and accused Hipkins of desperation.<ref name="Hanly election date" /> Similarly, Luxon defended his government's decision to limit voter enrollment to 12 days before the election date, arguing that it would reduce the time and work required to count special votes.<ref name="RNZ 19 Oct 2026" />

By 25 October, Le Quesne confirmed that over 132,000 New Zealanders based overseas had registered to vote, a "significant increase" from the 2023 general election. The Electoral Commission sent out 2.2 million emails to New Zealand expatriates overseas, followed by text reminders for those who did not open their emails. Radio New Zealand reported that some New Zealand expatriates had not receive text messages or emails from the Electoral Commission reminding them to enrol.<ref>{{cite news |title='I definitely didn't get an email': Expat fears overseas voters don't know about enrolment deadline |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2149030/i-definitely-didn-t-get-an-email-expat-fears-overseas-voters-don-t-know-about-enrolment-deadline |access-date=25 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=25 October 2026}}</ref>

== Election date and schedule ==
Unless an early election is called or the election date is set to circumvent holding a by-election, a general election is held every three years.  The last election was held on Saturday, 14 October 2023.<ref>{{cite web|title=New Zealand Election Results|url=http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/|publisher=Electoral Commission|accessdate=7 February 2024}}</ref>

The [[Governor-General of New Zealand|governor-general]] must issue [[writ of election|writs]] for an election within seven days of the expiration or [[dissolution of parliament|dissolution]] of the current Parliament.<ref>{{cite act|url=http://www.legislation.govt.nz/act/public/1993/0087/latest/DLM309425.html#DLM309425 |title=Electoral Act 1993 |at=s 125 |accessdate=7 February 2024 |archive-date=28 September 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110928000658/http://www.legislation.govt.nz/act/public/1993/0087/latest/DLM309425.html#DLM309425 |url-status=live }}</ref> Under section 17 of the [[Constitution Act 1986]], Parliament expires three years "from the day fixed for the return of the writs issued for the last preceding general election of members of the House of Representatives, and no longer." The writs for the 2023 election were returned on 9 November 2023.  As a result, the [[54th New Zealand Parliament|54th Parliament]] would expire, if not dissolved earlier, on Monday, 9 November 2026. Consequently, the last day for issuance of writs of election would be 16 November 2026. The writs must be returned within 60 days of their issuance (save for any judicial recount or death of a candidate), which would be Friday, 15 January 2027.<ref name="sec139">{{cite act|url=https://legislation.govt.nz/act/public/1993/0087/latest/DLM309482.html |title=Electoral Act 1993 |at=s 139 |access-date=22 January 2026 |archive-date=7 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241207212844/https://www.legislation.govt.nz/act/public/1993/0087/latest/DLM309482.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Because polling day must be a Saturday,<ref name="sec139" /> and two weeks is generally required for the counting of special votes, the last possible date that this election could be held is Saturday, 19 December 2026.

On 21 January 2026, Prime Minister Christopher Luxon announced that the election will be held on 7 November 2026.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/01/21/game-on-as-pm-luxon-reveals-2026-election-date/|title='Game on' as PM Luxon reveals 2026 election date|date=21 January 2026|access-date=21 January 2026|work=1 News|publisher=TVNZ}}</ref> The schedule for the election under legislation is as follows:<ref name="Website-1">{{Cite web |date=2026 |title=Key dates |url=https://vote.nz/2026-general-election/about/overview/key-dates |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260415030403/https://vote.nz/2026-general-election/about/overview/key-dates |archive-date=15 April 2026 |access-date=27 April 2026 |website=Vote NZ}}</ref>
