=== MPs not standing for re-election ===
[[Damien O'Connor]] announced on 26 January 2026 that he would not stand in the [[West Coast-Tasman]] electorate again, and subsequently transitioned to standing in the [[Waitaki (electorate)|Waitaki electorate]]. [[Andrew Bayly]] similarly announced on 3 March 2026 that he would not stand in the {{NZ electorate link|Port Waikato}} electorate again, and was exploring whether to stand on the party list.<ref>{{cite web |title=Former minister Bayly won't contest seat, looks to National list |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/03/03/former-minister-bayly-wont-contest-seat-looks-to-national-list/ |date=3 March 2026 |work=1News |access-date=3 March 2026}}</ref> He subsequently announced on 20 June he would not be seeking a place on the National list and would leave parliament at the election.<ref name="Ensor 20 Jun 2026">{{cite news|last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title= Former National minister Andrew Bayly to Leave Parliament, says he's decided not to seek list position |work = [[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=20 June 2026|url= https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/former-national-minister-andrew-bayly-to-leave-parliament-says-he-decided-not-to-seek-list-position/UXANABDU6BELBPTGLMOGMKTO3E/|access-date=20 June 2026}}</ref> 

[[Greg O'Connor (politician)|Greg O'Connor]] announced he would seek a list-only position after boundary changes disestablished his electorate of [[Ōhāriu (electorate)|Ōhāriu]]. On 8 June 2026, it was revealed he decided to not put his name forward for the Labour list.<ref>{{cite web |title=Greg O'Connor not standing after Labour list process that 'makes choosing Pope look transparent' |url=https://www.thepost.co.nz/politics/361018627/greg-oconnor-not-standing-after-labour-list-process-makes-choosing-pope-look-transparent |date=8 June 2026 |work=[[The Post (New Zealand newspaper)|The Post]] |access-date=8 June 2026}}</ref> Following an unsuccessful leadership challenge against Prime Minister [[Christopher Luxon]] which led to the loss of his ministerial portfolios on 12 August, [[Chris Penk]] announced he would not seek re-election.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/975747/chris-penk-says-he-will-not-seek-re-election-after-losing-ministerial-portfolios |title=Chris Penk says he will not seek re-election after losing ministerial portfolios |work=[[RNZ]] |date=12 August 2026 |access-date=27 September 2026}}</ref>

