In mid-October 2026, the Taxpayers' Union acknowledged using several [[artificial intelligence|AI]]-generated advertisements including one depicting Labour leader Chris Hipkins stealing wallets. The advocacy group defended the [[attack advertisement]], stating "we often use humour to make a serious and factual point, and this advert very much fits in that category."<ref>{{cite news |title=AI fear-based election messages finding an audience |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2047303/ai-fear-based-election-messages-finding-an-audience |access-date=25 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=21 October 2026}}</ref>

In late October 2026 the advocacy group Sensible Sentencing Trust released several billboards accusing Hipkins and Greens co-leaders Marama Davidson and Chlöe Swarbrick of supporting criminal gangs. These attack ads were produced by the New Zealand Taxpayers' Union leader [[Jordan Williams (New Zealand lawyer)|Jordan Williams]]' firm, the Campaign Company. In response, the Greens filed a complaint against the SST with the [[Advertising Standards Authority (New Zealand)|Advertising Standards Authority]], accusing the group of "impersonating" the party.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Crimp |first1=Lauren |title=Greens blast 'backing the gangs' election billboard ads |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/2173536/greens-blast-backing-the-gangs-election-billboard-ads |access-date=26 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=27 October 2026}}</ref> Following the complaint and media coverage, the SST removed images of the politicians from its attack advertisements but retained the message.<ref>{{cite news |title=Sensible Sentencing Trust removes images of politicians from attack advertisement |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2192780/sensible-sentencing-trust-removes-images-of-politicians-from-attack-advertisement |access-date=28 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=27 October 2026}}</ref>

===Party campaigns===
====National====
In November 2025, the National Party announced that it would gradually raise the default [[KiwiSaver]] contribution for employees and employers from 3% to 6%  by 2032 if re-elected in 2026.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hu |first1=Justin |title=National reveals new KiwiSaver boost as first election policy |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2025/11/23/national-reveals-new-kiwisaver-boost-as-first-election-policy/ |access-date=25 November 2025 |work=[[1News]] |date=23 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251125232950/https://www.1news.co.nz/2025/11/23/national-reveals-new-kiwisaver-boost-as-first-election-policy/ |archive-date=25 November 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 21 February 2026, National's conservation spokesperson [[Tama Potaka]] announced that the party would campaign on further restricting commercial fishing in the [[Hauraki Gulf]].<ref>{{cite news |title=National to campaign on review of 'controversial' Hauraki Gulf commercial fishing rules |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/587526/national-to-campaign-on-review-of-controversial-hauraki-gulf-commercial-fishing-rules |access-date=21 February 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=21 February 2026}}</ref> Following a [[cabinet reshuffle]] on 2 April 2026, cabinet minister [[Simeon Brown]] replaced [[Chris Bishop]] as National's campaign chair for the 2026 general election.<ref>{{cite news |title=Christopher Luxon reveals Cabinet shake-up |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/04/02/christopher-luxon-reveals-cabinet-shake-up/ |access-date=6 April 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=2 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260402141448/https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/04/02/christopher-luxon-reveals-cabinet-shake-up/ |archive-date=2 April 2026|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 17 May, Justice Minister [[Paul Goldsmith (politician)|Paul Goldsmith]] announced that National would campaign on scrapping "good character" sentencing discounts for sexual offending if re-elected.<ref>{{cite news |title=National pledges to scrap 'good character' sentencing discounts for sex offenders |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/05/17/national-pledges-to-scrap-good-character-sentencing-discounts-for-sex-offenders/ |access-date=17 May 2026 |work=[[1News]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260517052242/https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/05/17/national-pledges-to-scrap-good-character-sentencing-discounts-for-sex-offenders/ |archive-date=17 May 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> During the party's annual conference on 21 June, Luxon announced that National would make KiwiSaver compulsory for all workers and automatically enrol babies if re-elected.<ref>{{cite news |last=Hanly |first=Lillian |title=National to make KiwiSaver compulsory, if elected |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/609851/national-to-make-kiwisaver-compulsory-if-elected |access-date=22 June 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=21 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260621060414/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/609851/national-to-make-kiwisaver-compulsory-if-elected |archive-date=21 June 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 25 June, National announced it would introduce low-interest loans and planning reforms for installing home [[solar panels]], battery storage, heat pumps, insulation and energy upgrades. The loans would come from a fund supported by the [[New Zealand Crown]], local councils and the [[Local Government Funding Agency]].<ref>{{cite news |title=National pledges solar loans for homeowners, planning reforms |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/06/25/national-pledges-solar-loans-for-homeowners-planning-reforms/ |access-date=27 June 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=25 June 2026}}</ref>

On 2 July, National announced that it would prioritise free trade agreements with seven countries and using AI-powered tools to streamline export mechanisms.<ref>{{cite news |title=National promises to prioritise seven trade deals if elected |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/672751/national-promises-to-prioritise-seven-trade-deals-if-elected |access-date=5 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=5 July 2026}}</ref> On 19 July, Goldsmith announced that National would campaign on toughening sexual consent laws to clarify that silence was not consent. The opposition [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] and [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green]] parties criticised National for abandoning efforts to seek cross-party support on sexual consent legislation.<ref>{{cite news |title=National promises stricter rules on sexual consent |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/crime-and-justice/741708/national-promises-stricter-rules-on-sexual-consent |access-date=19 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=19 July 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260719041926/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/crime-and-justice/741708/national-promises-stricter-rules-on-sexual-consent |archive-date=19 July 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 26 July, National said it would campaign on extending parental leave from 26 to 30 weeks, and making it more flexible.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=National promises to extend parental leave from 26 weeks to 30, if re-elected |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/797174/national-promises-to-extend-paid-parental-leave-from-26-weeks-to-30-if-re-elected |access-date=26 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=26 July 2026}}</ref>  On 27 July, Luxon said that National would not work with the [[Opportunity Party (New Zealand)|Opportunity Party]], citing disagreement with Opportunity's policy of raising taxes.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=National rules out working with Opportunity Party |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/809555/national-rules-out-working-with-opportunity-party |access-date=27 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=27 July 2026}}</ref> A spokesperson for his office clarified that this ruled out any cooperation, including a [[confidence and supply]] arrangement.<ref>{{cite news |title=Christopher Luxon rules out working with Opportunity – but Opportunity says door is open |first=Henry |last=Cooke |date=27 July 2026 |url= https://www.thepost.co.nz/politics/361051248/christopher-luxon-rules-out-working-opportunity-opportunity-says-door-open |work=[[The Post (New Zealand newspaper)|The Post]] |access-date=30 August 2026}}</ref> On 24 August, Simeon Brown was emphatic that National would not work with Opportunity, and Luxon confirmed they would not work with them even in a confidence and supply agreement.<ref>{{cite news |title=Opportunity leader: Voters starting to see us as a stable and safe coalition partner |first=Giles |last=Dexter |date=24 August 2026 |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1093787/opportunity-leader-voters-starting-to-see-us-as-a-stable-and-safe-coalition-partner |work=RNZ |access-date=30 August 2026}}</ref>

On 6 August, Luxon announced that National would campaign on holding referendums on New Zealand's [[mixed member proportional]] (MMP) voting system and extending the Parliamentary term from three to four years.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Moir |first1=Jo |title=Christopher Luxon promises MMP referendum if re-elected, Winston Peters calls it a 'power grab' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/903205/christopher-luxon-promises-mmp-referendum-if-re-elected-winston-peters-calls-it-a-power-grab |access-date=6 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=6 August 2026}}</ref> On 8 August, National released its full list of election candidates.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McCulloch |first1=Craig |title=Watch: National Party announced list for 2026 General Election|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/934651/watch-national-party-announces-list-for-2026-general-election |access-date=9 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=8 August 2026}}</ref> The following day, the party said it would campaign on following three budget responsibility rules aimed at restoring the economy to surplus, cutting debt and low taxation.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Natanahira |first1=Tuwhenuaroa |title=National promises Budget Responsibility Rules if re-elected |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/945455/national-promises-budget-responsibility-rules-if-elected |access-date=9 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=9 August 2026}}</ref> On 23 August, Luxon confirmed that National would not introduce any new taxes if re-elected and ruled out plans to introduce a [[bed tax]] and [[bank tax]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hu |first1=Justin |title=Luxon vows 'no new taxes', dumping ideas for bank and bed tax |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/08/23/luxon-vows-no-new-taxes-dumping-ideas-for-bank-and-bed-tax/ |access-date=24 August 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=23 August 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260824064930/https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/08/23/luxon-vows-no-new-taxes-dumping-ideas-for-bank-and-bed-tax/ |archive-date=24 August 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 30 August, National announced that it would reduce domestic student loan repayment rates from 12% to 10% while increasing financial and legal penalties as well as interest rates for overseas-based borrowers in order to encourage more New Zealand expatriates to return from overseas.<ref>{{cite news |title=National vows to reduce student loan repayment rates if re-elected |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1175818/national-vows-to-reduce-student-loan-repayment-rate-if-re-elected |access-date=3 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=30 August 2026}}</ref>

On 6 September, National said it would campaign on making it easier for first-home buyers to access low deposit mortgages.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Coughlan |first1=Thomas |title=National pledges to make it easier for first home buyers to get 5% deposits|url=https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/news/politics/christopher-luxon-to-announce-new-national-party-housing-policy/ |access-date=8 September 2026 |work=[[Newstalk ZB]] |date=6 September 2026}}</ref> On 8 September, National announced that it would allocate funding from the International Visitor Levy to local councils as an alternative to the scrapped bed tax policy.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Moir |first1=Jo|title=Christopher announces National's alternative to introducing bed tax|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1300545/christopher-luxon-announces-national-s-alternative-to-introducing-bed-tax |access-date=8 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=8 September 2026}}</ref> On 13 September, National announced that it would establish an [[Auckland Transport]] police team to boost security on trains, ferries, buses, stations and transport hubs in Auckland.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Natanahira |first1=Tuwhenuaroa |title=National to set up Auckland Transport police team if re-elected |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1354691/national-to-set-up-auckland-transport-police-team-if-re-elected |access-date=17 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=13 September 2026}}</ref> On 16 September, National said it would campaign on splitting [[Foodstuffs (company)|Foodstuffs]] into two separate companies in order to break the country's [[Supermarkets in New Zealand|supermarket duopoly]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=McCulloch |first1=Craig |title=National vows to 'pursue' break up of supermarket giant Foodstuffs |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1410766/national-vows-to-pursue-break-up-of-supermarket-giant-foodstuffs |access-date=17 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=16 September 2026}}</ref> On 20 September, National confirmed they would campaign on banning "[[screen time]]" in early childhood centres and limiting screen usage for Years 1–3 pupils in primary schools.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1478528/national-vows-to-limit-screen-time-in-primary-schools-early-childhood-centres |title=National vows to limit screen time in primary schools, early childhood centres|date=20 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |access-date=20 September 2026}}</ref> 

National launched its election campaign on 27 September, with Luxon announcing that the party would make ten commitments including boosting economic growth, no new taxes, compulsory [[KiwiSaver]], extending paid parental leave, replacing the [[National Certificate of Educational Achievement]] (NCEA) and boosting roading, educational and health infrastructure.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=Election 2026: Christopher Luxon warns Labour bereft of ideas as he reveals National's 10 top promises |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-national-to-launch-campaign-with-speeches-from-john-key-christopher-luxon/QVP6QMNLZVA6NITNEQFXSGRFRU/ |access-date=28 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=27 September 2026}}</ref> On 1 October, National said it would campaign on trialing digital identification for trans-[[Tasman Sea|Tasman]] travel between New Zealand and Australia.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=National promises to make trans-Tasman travel easier |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1667774/national-promises-to-make-trans-tasman-travel-easier |access-date=3 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=1 October 2026}}</ref> On 2 October, Finance Minister [[Nicola Willis]] said the party would consider opening [[Kiwibank]] to local private capital in order to compete with Australian-owned banks operating in New Zealand.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dann |first1=Corin |title=National still keen on growing Kiwibank with the help of private local investors |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/business/1676357/national-still-keen-on-growing-kiwibank-with-the-help-of-private-local-investors |access-date=3 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=2 October 2026}}</ref> That same day, National released its nine-point energy plan to combat rising energy prices; with key policies including making it easier for households to install and sell rooftop solar energy, requiring data centres to have their own power generation and amending legislation to allow the construction of more private transmission infrastructure.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Coughlan |first1=Thomas |title=National pledges energy sector change to bring down power bills |url=https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/news/politics/election-2026-national-pivots-cost-of-living-focus-to-lines-companies-with-energy-policy/ |access-date=3 October 2026 |work=[[Newstalk ZB]] |date=2 October 2026}}</ref> 

On 5 October, National pledged to fully fund an extension to the [[Christchurch Northern Motorway]], at a cost of $800 million in the 2027 budget,<ref>{{cite news |title=National promises to fund $809 million Christchurch Northern Motorway Extension|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1737541/national-promises-to-fund-800-million-christchurch-northern-motorway-extension |access-date=5 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=5 October 2026}}</ref> a project that had already been promised in the 2023 election campaign. <ref>{{cite news |last1=Iasona |first1=Seni |title=Election 2023: National promises 13 new roads, better public transport in $24 billion transport package |url= https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/07/election-2023-national-promises-13-new-roads-better-public-transport-in-24-billion-transport-package.html |access-date=5 October 2026 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=31 July 2023 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230801014036/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/07/election-2023-national-promises-13-new-roads-better-public-transport-in-24-billion-transport-package.html |archive-date=1 August 2023}}</ref> On 6 October, National announced that it would invest $604 million in funding to boosting [[Pharmac]]'s medicine budget, expanding free bowel, cervical screening and breast screening by 2029. These health investments would be funded by reducing New Zealand's international aid budget outside the Pacific to 10 percent; a proposal that was criticised by Foreign Minister [[Winston Peters]] and Opposition leader [[Chris Hipkins]] but supported by Deputy Prime Minister [[David Seymour]].<ref>{{cite news |title=National says it will cut foreign aid to boost Pharmac medicines budget |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/10/06/national-says-it-will-cut-foreign-aid-to-boost-pharmac-medicines-budget/ |access-date=7 October 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=7 October 2026}}</ref> On 7 October, National released its small business plan, with a focus on cutting compliance costs and red tape.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=National unveils 47-point small business plan |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1779077/national-unveils-47-point-small-business-plan |access-date=7 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=7 October 2026}}</ref> 

On 8 October, National released its welfare policy, which proposed cutting $585 million in welfare spending. The party also proposed drug testing welfare beneficiaries with benefit sanctions for non-compliance.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Sam |last2=McConnell |first2=Glenn |title=National to introduce random drug testing for beneficiaries |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361043757/national-introduce-random-drug-testing-beneficiaries |access-date=9 October 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=8 October 2026}}</ref> On 9 October, National said it would allocate $300 million to provide low-cost loans to farmers for water storage.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McCulloch |first1=Craig |title=National woos farmer vote with $300m low-cost loans for water storage |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1803343/national-woos-farmer-vote-with-300m-low-cost-loans-for-water-storage |access-date=9 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=9 October 2026}}</ref> On 10 October, National said that it would review [[bottom trawling]] policies, establish a new marine reserve off [[Waiheke Island]] and expand the marine reserve near [[Goat Island (Auckland)|Goat Island]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Coughlan |first1=Thomas |title=National winks to Opportunity promising to review bottom trawling and create new Waiheke reserve |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/national-winks-to-opportunity-promising-to-review-bottom-trawling-and-create-new-waiheke-reserve/GZRZQIEUGFHHPHBR2SVTXKP6KU/ |access-date=12 October 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=10 October 2026}}</ref> On 11 October, National announced that it would campaign on requiring high school students to complete Year 11 before leaving school.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hu |first1=Justin |title=5000 students a year set to stay at school longer under National |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/10/11/5000-students-a-year-set-to-stay-at-school-longer-under-national/ |access-date=12 October 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=11 October 2026}}</ref>

On 12 October, Finance Minister Willis said that a re-elected National government's fiscal plan would focus on reducing debt and would delay introducing any new tax cuts.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hu |first1=Justin |title='Now is not the time': Tax cuts off the table, Nicola Willis says |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/10/12/now-is-not-the-time-tax-cuts-off-the-table-nicola-willis-says/ |access-date=13 October 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=12 October 2026}}</ref> On 13 October, National announced the five key commitments it would make during its first 100 days; namely lowering student loan repayment rates for New Zealanders living at home, widening access to five percent deposits for first-home buyers, extending paid parental leave from 26 to 30 weeks within three years, strengthening [[KiwiSaver]] programs, and fast-tracking work and visitor visas for partners of New Zealand citizens.<ref>{{cite news |title=National outlines policy commitments for its first 100 days if it leads next government |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1870656/national-outlines-policy-commitments-for-its-first-100-days-if-it-leads-next-government |access-date=15 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=13 October 2026}}</ref> On 14 October, transport spokesman Bishop released the party's Auckland plan, which included a pledge to remove the blanket bat on minimum car park requirements in city and district plans.<ref>{{cite news |title=National promises to remove ban on Auckland's minimum car parking rules |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1887747/national-promises-to-remove-ban-on-auckland-s-minimum-car-parking-rules |access-date=15 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=14 October 2026}}</ref> On 16 October, National pledged to simplify council regulations around agricultural and horticultural activities. <ref>{{cite news |last1=McCulloch |first1=Craig |title=National promises fewer council approvals for farms |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1952424/national-promises-fewer-council-approvals-for-farms |access-date=18 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=16 October 2026}}</ref> On 18 October, Justice Minister Goldsmith said that National would campaign on abolishing the legal "[[right to silence]]" in cases where children under the age of 12 were killed.<ref>{{cite news |title=National pledges to take away right to silence when a child is murdered |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1978144/national-pledges-to-take-away-right-to-silence-when-a-child-is-murdered |access-date=19 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=18 October 2026}}</ref>

On 21 October, Luxon released National's "Plan for Kiwi Kids" package, which included a three-year math acceleration programme, limited screen time among younger pupils, keeping the cellphone school ban, and scrapping NCEA.<ref>{{cite news |title=National promises to expand |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2051719/national-promises-to-expand-maths-acceleration-programme |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=21 October 2026}}</ref> On 22 October, National pledged to reserve 20% homes built under the Government's affordable housing scheme for pensioners.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McCulloch |first1=Craig |title=National promises more affordable homes for older people |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2074549/national-promises-more-affordable-homes-for-older-people |access-date=24 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=22 October 2026}}</ref> On 24 October, National pledged to introduce [[financial literacy]] into the school curriculum and to make it easier for children to open bank accounts.<ref>{{cite news |title=National pledges to add financial literacy to school curriculum in its 'Plan for Parents' policy |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2122803/national-pledges-to-add-financial-literacy-to-school-curriculum-in-its-plan-for-parents-policy |access-date=24 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=24 October 2026}}</ref>

====Labour====
In late October 2025, Labour made two policy announcements including the establishment of an economic investment fund called the "NZ Future Fund" and the introduction of a [[capital gains tax]] based on property transactions to subsidise doctors' visits.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Day |first1=Tom |title='NZ Future Fund': Labour reveals first major policy for next election |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2025/10/20/nz-future-fund-labour-reveals-first-major-policy-for-next-election/ |access-date=22 October 2025 |work=[[1News]] |date=20 October 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251021014300/https://www.1news.co.nz/2025/10/20/nz-future-fund-labour-reveals-first-major-policy-for-next-election/ |archive-date=21 October 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=McCulloch |first1=Craig |title=Labour to campaign on narrow capital gains tax, no wealth tax |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/577021/labour-to-campaign-on-narrow-capital-gains-tax-no-wealth-tax |access-date=1 November 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=28 October 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251029012513/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/577021/labour-to-campaign-on-narrow-capital-gains-tax-no-wealth-tax |archive-date=29 October 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> In early November 2025, the party proposed free [[cervical cancer]] screenings for all women aged between 25 and 69 years.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gabel |first1=Julia |title=Labour Party's latest election policy makes cervical screening free for all women aged 25–69 |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/570603/two-labour-bills-pass-into-law |access-date=7 November 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=6 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251107004636/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/labour-partys-latest-election-policy-makes-cervical-screening-free-for-all-women-aged-25-69/SEKM2YW245A7VKXA3WZCONPTLE/ |archive-date=7 November 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> In mid-November, the party announced that it would repeal the [[Regulatory Standards Act 2025]] within its first 100 days of government if it won the 2026 general election.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=Controversial Regulatory Standards Bill passes third reading |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/578793/controversial-regulatory-standards-bill-passes-third-reading |access-date=27 November 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=13 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251113200831/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/578793/controversial-regulatory-standards-bill-passes-third-reading |archive-date=13 November 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Prior to the party's annual general meeting, Labour leader [[Chris Hipkins]] confirmed that the party would contest all seven [[Māori electorates]], citing the internal conflict within [[Te Pāti Māori]], which holds six of those seats. The party has sought to exploit growing voter disillusionment with the National-led coalition government's handling of cost of living, health, economic and housing issues.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Anneke |title=Labour gathers for AGM as it shifts into campaign mode |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/580236/labour-gathers-for-agm-as-it-shifts-into-campaign-mode |access-date=27 November 2025 |date=28 November 2025}}</ref>

In mid January 2026, Labour proposed a streaming levy on foreign streaming companies like [[Netflix]] in order to invest in the [[New Zealand film industry]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Election 2026: Labour proposes 'game-changing' streaming levy to fund local productions |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/584114/election-2026-labour-proposes-game-changing-streaming-levy-to-fund-local-productions |access-date=15 January 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=15 January 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260115004649/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/584114/election-2026-labour-proposes-game-changing-streaming-levy-to-fund-local-productions |archive-date=15 January 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> In early February 2026, Labour leader [[Chris Hipkins]] along with the Greens co-leaders [[Chlöe Swarbrick]] and [[Marama Davidson]] announced during a press conference at the [[Waitangi Treaty Grounds]] that the two parties would work together during the election and in a future government.<ref>{{cite news|title=Watch Live: Labour and Greens speak from Waitangi Treaty grounds |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/alert-top/585809/watch-live-labour-and-greens-speak-from-waitangi-treaty-grounds |access-date=4 February 2026 |date=3 February 2026}}</ref> In late April, Labour announced that it would campaign on reinstating school boards' [[Treaty of Waitangi]] obligations and reversing the coalition government's curriculum changes, mandated testing and so-called "politicisation of the education system."<ref>{{cite web |last1=McCallum |first1=Hannah |title=Labour vows to reinstate school boards' Treaty obligations |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2026/04/28/labour-vows-to-reinstate-school-boards-treaty-obligations/ |website=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |access-date=3 May 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260428112508/https://newsroom.co.nz/2026/04/28/labour-vows-to-reinstate-school-boards-treaty-obligations/ |archive-date=28 April 2026 |date=28 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In mid-May 2026, the party declined to release details of its Future Funds policy until after the 2026 general election, including the cost to the [[New Zealand Crown]] and which state assets would be merged into the fund; citing several state-owned enterprises having [[Treaty of Waitangi]] obligations.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Coughlan |first1=Thomas |title=Labour admits it will not release 'cornerstone' policy costings and job modelling until after the election, citing Treaty of Waitangi concerns|url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/labour-admits-it-will-not-release-cornerstone-policy-costing-and-job-modelling-until-after-the-election-blames-treaty-of-waitangi-concerns/QNGXJK4SHZFOJB52WFCVHPLR4E/ |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=22 May 2026 |date=22 May 2026}}</ref> On 8 June, Labour released its party list for the 2026 election.<ref name="Palmer Labour list" /> [[Commissioner of Police (New Zealand)|Police Commissioner]] [[Richard Chambers (police officer)|Richard Chambers]] criticised Superintendent Rakesh Naidoo, the [[New Zealand Police]]'s ethnic, iwi and community relations manager, for not earlier disclosing his intention to stand as a Labour candidate, contending that this made him untenable for him to continue serving as a senior Police leadership role.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title='Untenable' for Labour's new candidate to continue as superintendent – Police Commissioner |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/597578/untenable-for-labour-s-new-candidate-to-continue-as-superintendent-police-commissioner |access-date=8 June 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=8 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260608063009/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/597578/untenable-for-labour-s-new-candidate-to-continue-as-superintendent-police-commissioner |archive-date=8 June 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> In response, Hipkins and Labour's police spokesperson [[Ginny Andersen]] defended Naidoo's candidacy.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=Labour police candidate stoush deepens as Police Minister accused of instructing Police Commissioner |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/labour-police-candidate-stoush-deepens-as-police-minister-accused-of-instructing-police-commissioner/Y7B4EK5MQZDIJNADWLJAYJ7O5Y/ |access-date=12 June 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=10 June 2026}}</ref>

On 10 June, Labour pledged to cap public transport fees in [[Auckland]], [[Wellington]] and [[Christchurch]] at $20 per week from July 2027, while a $10 cap would be rolled out in other regions and centres.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ricketts |first1=Emma |last2=McConnell |first2=Glenn |title=Labour reveals public transport fare cap in long-awaited election policy |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360990643/labour-reveals-public-transport-fare-cap-long-awaited-election-policy |access-date=12 June 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=10 June 2026}}</ref> While Labour estimated that its proposed public fares subsidy would cost $65 million a year, the economists Sam Warburton and [[Brad Olsen]] estimated that the programme would cost between $91 million and $112 million.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McCulloch |first1=Craig |last2=Hanly |first2=Lillian  |title='Material' miscalculation – economists identify apparent errors in Labour's transport policy |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/598268/material-miscalculation-economists-identify-apparent-errors-in-labour-s-transport-policy |access-date=21 June 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=16 June 2026}}</ref> On 16 June, Labour promised free maternity scans, as part of their Medicard proposals.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour promises free maternity scans if elected |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/598331/labour-promises-free-maternity-scans-if-elected |access-date=16 June 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=16 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260616120141/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/health/598331/labour-promises-free-maternity-scans-if-elected|archive-date=16 June 2026|url-status=live}}</ref> On 24 June, housing spokesperson [[Kieran McAnulty]] announced that Labour if elected would raise the Crown guarantee for community housing builders' social bonds.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Labour hopes new community underwrite scheme will incentivise more housing |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/618688/labour-hopes-new-community-underwrite-scheme-will-incentivise-more-housing |access-date=28 June 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=24 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260626171037/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/618688/labour-hopes-new-community-underwrite-scheme-will-incentivise-more-housing |archive-date=26 June 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 28 June, Hipkins proposed restoring the Apprenticeship Boost training scheme back to two years from 2028 and expanding the programme to include trades such as road construction, water treatment and hairdressing.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Election '26: Labour proposes Apprenticeship Boost Expansion |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/633845/election-26-labour-proposes-apprenticeship-boost-expansion |access-date=29 June 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=28 June 2026 }}</ref> On 8 July, Labour announced that it would introduce subsidies and two new loan schemes to reduce the cost of installing solar technology in homes, including low and middle-income households.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Labour promises subsidies, new loan schemes for installing solar |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/685297/labour-promises-subsidies-new-loan-schemes-for-installing-solar |access-date=9 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=8 July 2026}}</ref>

On 6 August, Labour released its small business package which included promising faster invoice payments, tax relief on new equipment and reduced compliance costs.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour unveils small business package promising faster repayments, tax relief |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/08/06/labour-unveils-small-business-package-promising-faster-repayments-tax-relief/ |access-date=6 August 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=6 August 2026}}</ref> On 9 August, Labour announced that it would campaign on improving access for small businesses to government contracts and reducing [[red tape]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ricketts |first1=Emma |title=Labour will open up government contracts to small businesses if elected|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361017794/labour-will-open-government-contracts-small-businesses-if-elected |access-date=9 August 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=9 August 2026}}</ref> On 23 August, Labour released its fiscal strategy; with key promises including reducing debt to 20 percent of [[gross domestic product]], establishing an independent Parliamentary Budget Office to scrutinise government spending and restoring well-being reporting.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Natanahira |first1=Tuwhenuaroa |title=Labour releases fiscal strategy, promises to slash debt|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1081146/labour-releases-fiscal-strategy-promises-to-slash-debt |access-date=23 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=23 August 2026}}</ref> The following day, Labour released its election slogan "Better Starts Now," with Hipkins stating that the party would campaign on changing the government.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour reveals its 2026 election campaign slogan |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/08/24/labour-reveals-its-2026-election-campaign-slogan/ |access-date=24 August 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=24 August 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260824065208/https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/08/24/labour-reveals-its-2026-election-campaign-slogan/ |archive-date=24 August 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 29 August, Labour pledged to establish a $21 million fund for Māori-led trades training programmes.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour pledged 21 million for Māori-led trades training|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1171950/labour-pledges-21m-for-maori-led-trades-training |access-date=3 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=29 August 2026}}</ref> On 31 August, Labour announced that it would campaign on guaranteeing a job for nursing graduates in order to boost staff numbers in the public health service.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Labour promises every nurse graduate will get a job |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1197296/labour-promises-every-graduate-nurse-will-get-a-job |access-date=3 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=31 August 2026}}</ref> That same day, Labour  said they would campaign on requiring retirement villages to compensate outgoing residents within three months of their departure.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1191902/labour-pledges-three-month-limit-for-retirement-village-repayments |title=Labour pledges three-month limit for retirement village repayments  |date=31 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |access-date=21 September 2026}}</ref>

On 13 September, Labour released its superannuation policies; which included making employer contributions to [[KiwiSaver]] compulsory, raising the minimum employee contribution to six percent, maintaining KiwiSaver contributions when parents undergo maternity leave, and banning new total renumeration contracts that absorb superannuation contributions from employees into salaries.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Labour to make employer contributions to KiwiSaver compulsory or elected |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1356180/labour-to-make-employer-contributions-to-kiwisaver-compulsory-if-elected |access-date=14 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=13 September 2026}}</ref> On 16 September, Hipkins said that Labour would campaign on banning major companies from charging excessive prices for goods and services.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour pledges to ban big companies from charging excessive prices |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1410372/labour-pledges-to-ban-big-companies-from-charging-excessive-prices |access-date=17 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=16 September 2026}}</ref> On 17 September, Deputy Leader [[Carmel Sepuloni]] said the party would campaign on extending the visa-free travel programme to [[Pacific Island Forum]] member states.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Selby |first1=Kaya |title=Labour promises visa-free travel for Pacific as overstayer rates rise |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/pacific/1431059/labour-promises-visa-free-travel-for-pacific-as-overstayer-rates-rise |access-date=17 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=17 September 2026}}</ref> That same day, Labour confirmed it would campaign on pausing some of the National-led government's educational changes including its plans to abolish the [[National Certificate of Educational Achievement]] (NCEA) and create an independent body for reviewing the school curriculum and subjects.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gerritsen |first1=John |title=Labour to pause government's school curriculum overhaul, NCEA changes if elected |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1438233/labour-to-pause-government-s-school-curriculum-overhaul-ncea-changes-if-elected |access-date=17 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=17 September 2026}}</ref> Labour also confirmed that it would repeal legislation enabling the [[Youth Court of New Zealand]] to send youth offenders to [[Boot camps in New Zealand|military style academies]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Williams |first1=Caroline |title='Failed experiment': Labour vows to scrap youth offender boot camps |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361034862/failed-experiment-labour-vows-scrap-youth-offender-boot-camps |access-date=19 September 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=17 September 2026}}</ref> On 18 September, Labour announced it would campaign on introducing a [[corporate homicide]] offence into law.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Crimp |first1=Lauren |title=Labour's proposed corporate homicide charges welcomed by Pike River mine families|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1446374/labour-s-proposed-corporate-homicide-charges-welcomed-by-pike-river-mine-families |access-date=19 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=18 September 2026}}</ref>

On 20 September, Labour announced it would campaign on forcing the two supermarket giants [[Foodstuffs (company)|Foodstuffs]] and [[Woolworths New Zealand]] to separate their retail and wholesale operations to help small grocers and encourage competition.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour proposes splitting Foodstuffs and Woolworths from their wholesale arms |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1478631/labour-proposes-splitting-foodstuffs-and-woolworths-from-their-wholesale-arms |access-date=20 September 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=20 September 2026}}</ref> On 21 September, Hipkins confirmed that Labour would not change the Government's interest deductibility rules for landlords if elected, adding that landlords would instead be covered by a [[capital gains tax]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hu |first1=Justin |title=Labour rules out interest deductibility changes for landlords |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/09/21/labour-rules-out-interest-deductibility-changes-for-landlords/ |access-date=25 September 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=21 September 2026}}</ref> On 23 September, foreign affairs spokesperson [[Vanushi Walters]] and leader Hipkins announced Labour's foreign policy; which emphasised respect for international law and the [[United Nations Charter]]. Labour also confirmed that it would recognise a Palestinian state, impose economic sanctions on Israel and support [[South Africa's genocide case against Israel]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Labour sums up its foreign policy approach as 'independent, principled and unafraid' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1528401/labour-sums-up-its-foreign-policy-approach-as-independent-principled-and-unafraid |access-date=28 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=23 September 2026}}</ref> On 24 September, Labour announced that it would invest NZ$4 billion in building new social housing, improving classrooms, repairing hospitals and job creation over the next four years.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Labour pledges $4 billion for state houses, school repairs and hospitals |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1552827/labour-pledges-4-billion-for-state-houses-school-repairs-and-hospitals |access-date=25 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=24 September 2026}}</ref> 

Labour launched its election campaign on 27 September, with Hipkins promising that the Government would forgive 10 percent of eligible [[student loan]]s at a cost of $583.4 million over the next four years.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=Labour reveals policy to forgive student loan debt as party kicks off election campaign|url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/labour-kicks-off-campaign-after-national-hammers-home-tax-attack/JJ6SXVR3HJH2RB7BF4567UTSDI/ |access-date=28 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=27 September 2026}}</ref> On 1 October, Labour pledged to introduce a new "fair go law" to ban businesses from charging surprise fees, "worthless" insurance, hard to cancel subscriptions and "unreasonable" exit fees across a range of products and services.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour promises law to stop businesses 'tricking' customers with hidden costs and surprise fees |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1667731/labour-promises-law-to-stop-businesses-tricking-customers-with-hidden-costs-and-surprise-fees |access-date=3 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=1 October 2026}}</ref> On 4 October, Labour announced it would campaign on restoring the former [[pay equity]] regime but declined to provide a specific figure for reinstating the programme.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Coughlan |first1=Thomas |title=Labour promises to reinstate pay equity, but only costs one claim, leaving $8.5b out of plan |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/labour-makes-election-announcement-as-campaign-ramps-up/X3MLYPADOVETPOWTTB5O76U74M/ |access-date=4 October 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=4 October 2026}}</ref> Labour promised to immediately pay 65,000 care and support workers over the next four years, costed at $2.5 billion.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Labour releases its fiscal plan, promises pay rise for care and support workers|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1718141/labour-releases-its-fiscal-plan-promises-pay-rise-for-care-and-support-workers |access-date=4 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=4 October 2026}}</ref>

On 5 October, Labour pledged to introduce a "right to repair" car manufacturing policy and strengthen scrutiny of fuel prices  to combat overpricing.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour targets fuel prices, right to repair for drivers |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/10/05/labour-targets-fuel-prices-right-to-repair-for-drivers/ |access-date=5 October 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=5 October 2026}}</ref> That same day, Labour said it would campaign on making international musicians touring New Zealand to include a local supporting act in order to support the New Zealand music industry.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Sam |title=Labour vows to make international touring artists have a local supporting act when visiting NZ |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361042546/labour-vows-make-international-touring-artists-have-local-support-act-when-playing-nz |access-date=7 October 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=5 October 2026}}</ref> On 7 October, Labour pledged to make it easier for new banking companies to compete with major Australian-owned banks including [[ANZ Bank New Zealand]], [[ASB Bank]], [[Bank of New Zealand]] and [[Westpac New Zealand]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=McCulloch |first1=Craig |title=Labour targets big banks in cost-of-living push |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1780500/labour-targets-big-banks-in-cost-of-living-push |access-date=7 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=7 October 2026}}</ref> On 8 October, Labour campaigned on forcing airlines to advertise their "real prices" and to compensate passengers for cancelled flights or significant changes to travel itineraries.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour says it will force airlines to show real prices up front |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1790464/labour-says-it-will-force-airlines-to-show-real-prices-up-front |access-date=9 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=8 October 2026}}</ref>

On 11 October, Hipkins confirmed that Labour would campaign on restoring [[School lunches in New Zealand|locally-made school lunches]] and rural school bus routes.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hu |first1=Justin |title=Hipkins promises end to 'slop' lunches, reveals 2020 tax support |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/10/11/hipkins-promises-end-to-slop-lunches-reveals-2020-tax-support/ |access-date=12 October 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=11 October 2026}}</ref> On 12 October, Labour pledged to reduce the cost of school uniforms by limiting the number of branded uniform items families had to buy, and allowing more retailers to stock them.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour announces policy for cheaper school uniforms |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/10/12/labour-announces-policy-for-cheaper-school-uniforms/ |access-date=12 October 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=12 October 2026}}</ref> On 18 October, Labour to allocate $7 million a year into sports and recreational programmes for children and youths to combat Internet addiction.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour pledges $7 million to get kids off screens and 'back to being kids' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1985304/labour-pledges-7-million-to-get-kids-off-screens-and-back-to-being-kids |access-date=18 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=18 October 2026}}</ref> 

On 19 October, Labour confirmed it would seek to progress an under-16 social media ban to select committee level within the first 100 days of being elected into government.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour pledges social media ban for under-16s |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2015458/labour-pledges-social-media-ban-for-under-16s |access-date=18 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=19 October 2026}}</ref> On 20 October, Labour pledged to improve the quality of services in hospital emergency departments.<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour promises shorter ED wait times, immediate review of unused hospital beds |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2033653/labour-promises-shorter-ed-wait-times-immediate-review-of-unused-hospital-beds |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=20 October 2026}}</ref> On 22 October, education spokesperson Andersen confirmed that Labour would campaign on hiring 1,000 more teacher aides at a cost of $80 million over the next four years.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=Labour promises to hire 1000 more teacher aides |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2074975/labour-promises-to-hire-1000-more-teacher-aides |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=22 October 2026}}</ref> 

On 28 October, Labour announced its social housing policy, with key promises including banning "no-cause" [[eviction]]s, making rental homes warmer and safer and strengthening tenancy rights.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title='Renters deserve a fair deal' - Labour promises to ban no-cause evictions |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2205040/renters-deserve-a-fair-deal-labour-promises-to-ban-no-cause-evictions |access-date=28 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=28 October 2026}}</ref> On 29 October, Labour released its 2026 election manifesto, entitled "What Better Means for New Zealand," which collated its election policy announcements.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Labour releases manifesto collating policies ahead of election |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/2220227/labour-releases-manifesto-collating-policies-ahead-of-election |access-date=29 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=29 October 2026}}</ref>

====Green====
In May 2025, the Greens released their alternative "Green Budget", which proposed investing $8 billion over the next four years in various green policies including creating a Ministry of Green Works and supporting sustainable infrastructure.<ref>{{cite news |title=Green Party unveils new scheme promising to create thousands of 'green jobs' |url=https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/on-air/heather-du-plessis-allan-drive/audio/chloee-swarbrick-green-party-co-leader-on-the-partys-plan-to-create-thousands-of-green-jobs/ |access-date=1 May 2025 |work=[[Newstalk ZB]] |date=1 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250501225606/https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/on-air/heather-du-plessis-allan-drive/audio/chloee-swarbrick-green-party-co-leader-on-the-partys-plan-to-create-thousands-of-green-jobs/ |archive-date=1 May 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Other notable Green Budget policies have included a new [[wealth tax]], higher corporate taxes, a private jet tax, higher mining royalties and income tax rates.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Daalder |first1=Marc |title=Greens try to set agenda on pre-Budget tax debate |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2025/05/20/greens-try-to-set-agenda-on-pre-budget-tax-debate/ |access-date=24 May 2025 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=20 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250519215336/https://newsroom.co.nz/2025/05/20/greens-try-to-set-agenda-on-pre-budget-tax-debate/ |archive-date=19 May 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> In mid November 2025, the Greens confirmed they would revoke all coal, gold and seabed mining consents approved under the incumbent coalition government's [[Fast-track Approvals Act 2024]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gabel |first1=Julia |title='On notice': Green Party unveils mining policy ahead of 2026 general election |url=https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/news/national/on-notice-green-party-unveils-mining-policy-ahead-of-2026-general-election/ |access-date=17 November 2025 |work=[[Newstalk ZB]] |date=17 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251117053812/https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/news/national/on-notice-green-party-unveils-mining-policy-ahead-of-2026-general-election/ |archive-date=17 November 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In early February 2026, co-leader [[Marama Davidson]] confirmed that the party would be fielding candidates in three of the [[Māori electorates]] including list MP [[Huhana Lyndon]], lawyer Tania Waikato and former Te Pāti Māori candidate Heather Te-Au Skipworth.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=Waitangi wrap: Speeches, celebrations and heckling |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/586080/waitangi-wrap-speeches-celebrations-and-heckling |access-date=7 February 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=5 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260205093314/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/586080/waitangi-wrap-speeches-celebrations-and-heckling |archive-date=5 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 12 March, the party released its draft party list of candidates, which was finalised on 22 April.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=Election 2026: Green Party MPs suffer big drops in draft list, Tamatha Paul ranked high as party hypes Māori candidates |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-green-party-mps-suffer-big-drops-in-draft-list-tamatha-paul-ranked-high-as-party-hypes-maori-candidates/3LDIXF7JH5C2JPN7M4II6L2CO4/ |access-date=12 March 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=12 March 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Hu |first1=Justin |title=Green Party confirm election list candidates |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/04/22/green-party-confirm-election-list-candidates/ |access-date=6 May 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=22 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260422183415/https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/04/22/green-party-confirm-election-list-candidates/ |archive-date=22 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 25 March, the party announced its housing policies including building 40,000 new social homes over a five-year period, passing a Renters' Rights Bill capping [[renting|rent]] increases at 2% a year, and creating a national register of [[landlord]]s.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=Green Party election housing policy: Rent increase caps, a rental WoF and tens of thousands of new public homes |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/green-party-election-housing-policy-rent-increase-caps-a-rental-wof-and-tens-of-thousands-of-new-public-homes/6BHQQUJBEFBWVKLYH6BOND5JNE/ |access-date=25 March 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=25 March 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260325025312/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/green-party-election-housing-policy-rent-increase-caps-a-rental-wof-and-tens-of-thousands-of-new-public-homes/6BHQQUJBEFBWVKLYH6BOND5JNE/ |archive-date=25 March 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> During the party's State of the Planet annual address on 19 April, Davidson and Swarbrick confirmed that the Greens would campaign on promoting mass electrification at the 2026 election. Swarbrick said that if elected, the Greens would prioritise solar, wind, water and geothermal energy over fossil fuels in response to the [[2026 Iran War]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Greens to campaign on mass electrification after Iran war chokes fuel supplies |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/electrify-everything-greens-to-campaign-on-mass-electrification-after-iran-conflict-chokeholds-fuel-supplies/LLYPQJECONAUHGC2HX4F5VVIS4/ |access-date=19 April 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=19 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260419230648/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/electrify-everything-greens-to-campaign-on-mass-electrification-after-iran-conflict-chokeholds-fuel-supplies/LLYPQJECONAUHGC2HX4F5VVIS4/ |archive-date=19 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 21 June, the party's co-leaders announced a range of wealth, inheritance and corporate taxes to "tackle inequality and corporate greed." They also proposed a tax-free threshold for incomes below $10,000 while raising the income tax rate for those earning over $160,000.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Greens propose wealth, corporate, and inheritance taxes to fund income tax changes |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/609649/greens-propose-wealth-corporate-and-inheritance-taxes-to-fund-income-tax-changes |access-date=22 June 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=21 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260621092249/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/609649/greens-propose-wealth-corporate-and-inheritance-taxes-to-fund-income-tax-changes |archive-date=21 June 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Due to human error, the Greens' tax reform policy was accidentally released early on the party's website several hours prior to the Sunday press conference.<ref>{{cite news |title=Election 2026: Greens' tax plan released online early, sees party dramatically rein in ambitions |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-greens-tax-plan-released-online-early-sees-party-dramatically-rein-in-ambitions/Q2KBEETURNB3TOC6MRS5K3GMXM/ |access-date=22 June 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=21 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260621032415/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-greens-tax-plan-released-online-early-sees-party-dramatically-rein-in-ambitions/Q2KBEETURNB3TOC6MRS5K3GMXM/ |archive-date=21 June 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> The following day, the party corrected the economic costings for its tax reform policy by $800 million after the [[Inland Revenue Department (New Zealand)|IRD]] administration cost was recorded in the wrong place.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hu |first1=Justin |title=Greens correct $800m tax policy error, Swarbrick calls it a 'typo' |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/06/22/greens-correct-800m-tax-policy-error-swarbrick-calls-it-a-typo/ |access-date=22 June 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=22 June 2026}}</ref> In response to the Greens' taxation policy, the National Party released a social media video alleging that the Greens were seeking to tax 45 percent of incomes above $160,000. Swarbrick criticised the social media video as an inaccurate misrepresentation of her party's tax policy and called on National's social media team to remove the video. The National Party declined to take down the video.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title='AI slop' – Greens criticise National attack ad |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/650807/ai-slop-greens-criticise-national-attack-ad |access-date=4 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=1 July 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260702191224/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/650807/ai-slop-greens-criticise-national-attack-ad |archive-date=2 July 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 6 July, the Greens released their "Drink, Swim, Fish" policy, which proposed phasing out synthetic [[nitrogen fertiliser]]s, reducing the number of [[cows]] and [[sheep]] to improve river water quality, and protecting at least 30% of the ocean to restore marine ecosystems.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gabel |first1=Julia |title=Election 2026: Green Party releases drinking water, oceans and rivers policy |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-green-party-releases-drinking-water-oceans-and-rivers-policy/V3QS5ZKVLZGHHBKDRSQHRKYJEM/ |access-date=7 July 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=6 July 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260706034258/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-green-party-releases-drinking-water-oceans-and-rivers-policy/V3QS5ZKVLZGHHBKDRSQHRKYJEM/ |archive-date=6 July 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 16 July, the Greens announced that they would campaign on introducing automatic union membership for new employees.<ref>{{cite news |title=Green Party to take automatic union membership bill to the election |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361006822/green-party-take-automatic-union-membership-bill-election |access-date=17 July 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=16 July 2026}}</ref> On 20 July, the Greens released their energy policy which included reducing energy bills by creating a new [[Crown entity]] called Kiwipower to invest in renewable energy and address the country's "dry year" problem. They also proposed zero-interest loans for solar and battery installations, legal plug-in solar panels, a renters' right to panels, and expanding the solar buildout for public homes.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hu |first1=Justin |title=Greens pledge 'Kiwipower' to boost renewables and cut power bills|url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/07/20/greens-pledge-kiwipower-to-boost-renewables-and-cut-power-bills/ |access-date=20 July 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=20 July 2026}}</ref> On 26 July, the Greens released their election manifesto and campaign slogan "For All of Us." Swarbrick said the party would campaign on taxing the rich and basic human rights including food, shelter and jobs. She also reiterated calls for a one-year pause on consents for new AI [[data centre]]s.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Natanahira |first1=Tuwhenuaroa |title=Greens unveil manifesto at packed AGM in Auckland|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/799663/greens-unveil-election-manifesto-at-packed-agm-in-auckland |access-date=26 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=26 July 2026}}</ref>

On 9 August, the Greens campaigned on establishing a $2.4 billion trust dedicated to supporting [[marae]] as "critical infrastructure" during weather events and other disasters.<ref>{{cite news |title=Greens propose $2.4b funding to back 'critical' marae|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/945925/greens-propose-2-point-4b-funding-to-back-critical-marae |access-date=10 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=9 August 2026}}</ref> On 20 August, the Greens announced they would campaign on restoring the [[Sixth Labour Government of New Zealand|previous Labour Government]]'s [[School lunches in New Zealand|school lunch programme]] and would seek to expand it to 400 other schools.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gabel |first1=Julia |title=Election 2026: Greens promise to restore and expand previous free school lunches scheme |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/greens-promise-to-restore-and-expand-previous-free-school-lunches-scheme/2VT7XN74AZDQ3JP6VYMNWRYVUA/ |access-date=21 August 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=20 August 2026}}</ref> 

On 6 September, the Greens announced that they would create a government-owned supermarket chain called "KiwiMart" in order to break the country's [[Supermarkets in New Zealand|supermarket duopoly]]. This policy would involve forcing [[Foodstuffs (company)|Foodstuffs]] and [[Woolworths (New Zealand)|Woolworths]] to sell 120 of their supermarkets and two distribution centres to the new KiwiMart entity.<ref>{{cite news |title=Greens propose new supermarket chain in bid to cut grocery costs |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/09/06/greens-propose-new-supermarket-chain-in-bid-to-cut-grocery-costs/ |access-date=9 September 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=6 September 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260906070138/https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/09/06/greens-propose-new-supermarket-chain-in-bid-to-cut-grocery-costs/ |archive-date=6 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 14 September, the party announced that they would invest $200 million into various [[Māori language]] initiatives including teacher training, the [[Te Taura Whiri i te Reo Māori]] (Māori Language Commission) and the Māori language development and support agency [[Te Mātāwai]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Greens promise $200m into te reo Māori initiatives |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1364436/greens-promise-200m-into-te-reo-maori-initiatives |access-date=17 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=14 September 2026}}</ref> On 20 September, the Greens said they would campaign on amending the law to allow market rent reviews to lower business rents, and allowing local councils to run  rental auctions for spaces that had been vacant for over 12 months.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1478765/greens-announce-reforms-to-lower-commercial-rents-fill-empty-shops |title=Greens announced reforms to lower commercial rents, fill empty shops  |date=20 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |access-date=20 September 2026}}</ref> On 22 September, the Greens announced they would campaign on capping political donations at NZ$30,000, prevent companies from donating, set up a lobbying public register and implement a two-year standing down period for ministers wanting to become lobbyists.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=Election 2026: Greens calling for donations cap and register of lobbyists in new policy |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-greens-calling-for-donations-cap-and-register-of-lobbyists-in-new-policy/52UOAQIMLBAAVEVK4SFO7VC53E/ |access-date=25 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=22 September 2026}}</ref>

On 1 October, the Greens said they would campaign on restoring the previous Labour Government's pay equity scheme and invest at least $4.5 billion to fund a settlement for 65,000 care and support workers affected by the National-led government's [[Equal Pay Amendment Act 2025|abrogation of the scheme]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Greens put $4.5b bill on first part of reversing government's pay equity changes |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1671503/greens-put-4-point-5b-bill-on-first-part-of-reversing-government-s-pay-equity-changes |access-date=3 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=1 October 2026}}</ref> On 3 October, the Greens said they will connect the country with [[Inter-city rail|intercity rail]], costing $4 billion: introducing an overnight service between Auckland and Wellington; a passenger service between Christchurch and Dunedin; and extending the [[Te Huia|Te Huia service]] to Tauranga.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Greens' $4b rail plan: Overnight trains, Christchurch-Dunedin service |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/10/03/greens-4b-rail-plan-overnight-trains-christchurch-dunedin-service/ |access-date=4 October 2026 |date=3 October 2026 |work=[[1News]] |language=en}}</ref> On 4 October, the Greens pledged to make public transportation free for children, students, apprentices, Community Service Card holders, and Total mobility users, while reducing transport fees to $2 for everyone else.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Crimp |first1=Lauren |title=Greens eye free, fast and reliable public transport with economic promises|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1714650/greens-eye-free-fast-and-reliable-public-transport-with-election-promises |access-date=4 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=4 October 2026}}</ref>

On 5 October, the Greens announced that they would campaign on removing level crossings on Auckland's [[Western Line (Auckland)|Western Line]] railway line to improve safety, at a cost of $2.5–3.5 billion.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bradford |first1=Katie |title=Election 2026: Greens shriek in horror as car races through safety barrier while announcing a rail safety pledge |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/election-2026-greens-shriek-in-horror-as-car-races-through-safety-barrier-while-announcing-a-rail-safety-pledge/WC3RRDD4ZZFSJPZNX4WZVNUCFQ/ |access-date=5 October 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=5 October 2026}}</ref> On 12 October, the Greens pledged to regulate [[artificial intelligence]] with the goal of protecting New Zealand creativity, data and jobs.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Sam |title='We don't work for AI': Greens vow regulation of artificial intelligence for 'public good' |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361045256/we-dont-work-ai-greens-vows-regulation-artificial-intelligence-public-good |access-date=13 October 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=12 October 2026}}</ref> On 13 October, the Greens released their economic development plan, which proposed creating 284,000 jobs over the next four years at a cost of nearly $6 billion.<ref>{{cite news |title=Greens promise 284,000 jobs with $6b economic plan |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1867430/greens-promise-284-000-jobs-with-6b-economic-plan |access-date=13 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=13 October 2026}}</ref> On 14 October, Swarbrick pledged that the Greens would give local councils the right to enact [[bed tax|bed levies]] to fund infrastructure if elected.<ref>{{cite news |title=Greens promise to give councils power to set up bed levy |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1898168/greens-promise-to-give-councils-power-to-set-up-bed-levy |access-date=16 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=14 October 2026}}</ref> On 15 October, the Greens pledged to reverse the National-led government's $2.4 billion tax cuts to the public service, at a cost of $3.17 billion over the next four years.<ref>{{cite news |title=Greens vow to reverse National's $2.4b public service cuts at cost of $3.17b |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1921798/greens-vow-to-reverse-national-s-2-point-4b-public-service-cuts-at-cost-of-3-point-17b |access-date=16 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=15 October 2026}}</ref>

On 16 October, the Greens said they would introduce universal payments for trainee teachers, nurses, veterinarians, dentists and social support workers in order to encourage them to complete their studies.<ref>{{cite news |title=Greens promise universal payment for trainee teachers, nurses, vets and dentists |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1950949/greens-promise-universal-payment-for-trainee-teachers-nurses-vets-and-dentists |access-date=18 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=16 October 2026}}</ref> On 17 October, the Greens pledged to abolish the [[minimum wage|training wage]] (which is currently set at NZ$19.16) and invest NZ$30 million in [[Māori people|Māori]] and [[Pasifika New Zealanders|Pasifika]]-led training programmes.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Greens propose to abolish training wage |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1968685/greens-propose-to-abolish-training-wage |access-date=18 October 2026 |date=17 October 2026}}</ref> On 23 October, the Greens released their fiscal plan, which proposed increasing health spending by NZ$3 billion per year over the next four budgets, at a total cost of NZ$30 billion over the full period.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=Green Party releases fiscal plan, promises to increase health spending by $3 billion each year |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2097318/green-party-releases-fiscal-plan-promises-to-increase-health-spending-by-3-billion-each-year |access-date=24 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=23 October 2026}}</ref>

====ACT====
On 15 February 2026, the ACT Party held its annual State of the Nation address in Christchurch, where party leader David Seymour stated they would campaign on decreasing government spending, and merging ministerial portfolios.<ref>{{cite video |url=https://www.youtube.com/live/DWsHbI5PywY |title=ACT Party State of the Nation 2026 – Full Address |date=14 February 2026 |publisher=ACT New Zealand |location=Christchurch, New Zealand |people=David Seymour}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=ACT leader David Seymour delivers 'State of the Nation' speech |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/586906/act-leader-david-seymour-delivers-state-of-the-nation-speech |access-date=15 February 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=15 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260215015105/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/586906/act-leader-david-seymour-delivers-state-of-the-nation-speech |archive-date=15 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 19 February, Seymour advocated merging the [[Ministry for Ethnic Communities]], [[Ministry for Women]], [[Ministry for Pacific Peoples]], Office for Seniors, [[Ministry of Youth Development (New Zealand)|Ministry of Youth Development]] and [[Te Puni Kōkiri]] (Ministry for Māori Development) into the [[Ministry for Culture and Heritage]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title='More coherent': Seymour reveals first ministries he would axe |url=https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/news/politics/act-leader-david-seymour-reveals-first-ministries-he-would-axe-to-reduce-size-of-government/ |access-date=19 February 2026 |work=[[Newstalk ZB]] |date=19 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260219023519/https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/news/politics/act-leader-david-seymour-reveals-first-ministries-he-would-axe-to-reduce-size-of-government/ |archive-date=19 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 16 April, Seymour confirmed that ACT would campaign on banning unelected appointees on local council committees from having voting rights. [[ACT Local]] councillor Davina Smolders had earlier clashed with [[Mayor of the Far North]] [[Moko Tepania]] for expanding the number of [[Māori people|Māori]] [[iwi]] (tribal) and [[hapu]] (clan) representatives on the [[Far North District Council]]'s Te Kuaka Māori Strategic Relationships Committee.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=ACT would ban voting rights for unelected appointees on local council committees if elected |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/top/592583/act-would-ban-voting-rights-for-unelected-appointees-on-local-council-committees-if-elected |access-date=19 April 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=16 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260419224621/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/top/592583/act-would-ban-voting-rights-for-unelected-appointees-on-local-council-committees-if-elected |archive-date=19 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 3 May, Seymour released ACT's immigration policies, which included deporting serious non-citizen offenders, a five-year welfare exclusion period for all residency visa holders, a $6 daily infrastructure surcharge for temporary work visa holders, strengthening English language requirements for most work visa holders, and creating a new overstayer enforcement unit within [[Immigration New Zealand]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gabel |first1=Julie |title=Election 2026: ACT reveals immigration campaign policies |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-act-reveals-immigration-campaign-polices/WTABGHRF5JACTK2HZ5AEH74EJ4/ |access-date=4 May 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=3 May 2026}}</ref> On 5 May, Seymour said ACT would campaign on allowing pharmacists to treat and manage several medical issues in order to reduce the workload on [[general practitioner]]s and [[nurse]]s.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McConnell |first1=Glenn |title=Seymour says pharmacists should treat more, so you don't need to see a GP |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360974444/seymour-says-pharmacists-should-treat-more-so-you-dont-need-see-gp |access-date=7 May 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=6 May 2026}}</ref>

On 5 June, ACT's education spokesperson [[Laura McClure]] confirmed the party would campaign on reducing the cost of [[school uniform]]s.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Crimp |first1=Lauren |title=ACT to campaign on reducing 'absolutely ridiculous' cost of school uniforms |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/597345/act-to-campaign-on-reducing-absolutely-ridiculous-cost-of-school-uniforms |access-date=8 June 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=5 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260607102603/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/597345/act-to-campaign-on-reducing-absolutely-ridiculous-cost-of-school-uniforms |archive-date=7 June 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 9 June, the party's agricultural spokesperson [[Andrew Hoggard]] proposed a new Rural Workforce Visa to provide farmers and other rural employers with a year-round supply of workers.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT proposes Rural Workforce Visa to address farm labour shortages |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/06/09/act-proposes-rural-workforce-visa-to-address-farm-labour-shortages/ |access-date=13 June 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=9 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260613042327/https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/06/09/act-proposes-rural-workforce-visa-to-address-farm-labour-shortages/|archive-date=13 June 2026|url-status=live}}</ref> On 10 June, ACT proposed a "split-gas" emissions target to ease restrictions on so-called "short-lived gases" like [[methane]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=ACT proposes new 'split-gas' emissions target |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/597787/act-proposes-new-split-gas-emissions-target |access-date=13 June 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=10 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260610021059/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/597787/act-proposes-new-split-gas-emissions-target |archive-date=10 June 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 29 June, ACT proposed making it mandatory for health beneficiaries to get medical certificates issued by [[Ministry of Social Development (New Zealand)|Ministry of Social Development]]-approved doctors.<ref>{{cite news |title='No one wants to be sick forever' – ACT Party's new welfare policy slammed |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/641860/no-one-wants-to-be-sick-forever-act-party-s-new-welfare-policy-slammed |access-date=30 June 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=29 June 2026}}</ref> On 30 June, ACT's [[Porirua (New Zealand electorate)|Porirua]] candidate Lyra Yan Zhang resigned from the party's candidate list after failing to declare her previous membership of the [[China Zhi Gong Party]], which is aligned with the [[Chinese Communist Party]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Act candidate resigns after Chinese political group link revealed |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/act-candidate-resigns-after-chinese-political-group-link-revealed/KJRRJLISUVHHVBZ3Z24P4FURQQ/ |access-date=30 June 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=30 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260630152311/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/act-candidate-resigns-after-chinese-political-group-link-revealed/KJRRJLISUVHHVBZ3Z24P4FURQQ/ |archive-date=30 June 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 2 July, the party's family and sexual violence spokesperson [[Karen Chhour]] announced that ACT would seek to amend the [[Crimes Act 1961]] to provide greater protections for pets and their owners against coercion and domestic violence.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Natanahira |first1=Tuwhenuaroa |title=ACT policy aims to stop abusers 'weaponising' pets to control victims |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/673624/act-policy-aims-to-stop-abusers-weaponising-pets-to-control-victims |access-date=5 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=5 July 2026}}</ref> On 8 July, deputy leader [[Nicole McKee]] announced that ACT would campaign on adding [[burglary]] to the [[three-strikes law|three-strikes sentencing regime]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Coughlan |first1=Thomas |title=Act Party to campaign on new three strikes regime for burglary |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/act-party-to-campaign-on-new-three-strikes-regime-for-burglary/MW2SB3L6U5AXBLY55A75WVMYQ4/ |access-date=9 July 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=8 July 2026}}</ref> In late July, ACT announced that it would campaign on transferring responsibility for game animals on conservation land from the [[Department of Conservation (New Zealand)|Department of Conservation]] to the [[Game Animal Council]], giving hunters a stake in managing the country's conservation estate.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT wants hunters in charge of game animals on conservation land, not DOC |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/793521/act-wants-hunters-in-charge-of-game-animals-on-conservation-land-not-doc |access-date=25 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=25 July 2026}}</ref> On 26 July, ACT announced it would campaign on boosting health spending including raising [[Pharmac]]'s budget to 13% by 2033.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=ACT campaigns to close the Pharmac funding gap |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/799139/act-campaigns-to-close-the-pharmac-funding-gap |access-date=26 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=26 July 2026}}</ref>

On 2 August, ACT candidate [[Paul Henry (broadcaster)|Paul Henry]] said the party would campaign on amending the law to give teachers "reasonable force" to remove disruptive students from the classroom.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Natanahira |first1=Tuwhenuaroa |title=ACT candidate Paul Henry unveils plan to let teachers use 'reasonable force' on students |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/860702/act-candidate-paul-henry-unveils-plan-to-let-teachers-use-reasonable-force-on-students |access-date=3 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=2 August 2026}}</ref> On 14 August, the party campaigned on introducing a "local tourism dividend" to support local councils as an alternative to a proposed [[bed tax]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Crimp |first1=Lauren |title='Local tourism dividend': ACT Party proposes bed tax alternative |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/999428/local-tourism-dividend-act-party-proposes-bed-tax-alternative |access-date=17 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=14 August 2026}}</ref> On 16 August, ACT confirmed it would campaign on amending public service legislation to end so-called "race-based hiring" in favour of resetting government agencies along the principles of merit and equal treatment.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT pledges to end 'race-based hiring' in public service 'reset' |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361020870/act-pledges-end-race-based-hiring-public-service-reset |access-date=21 August 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=16 August 2026}}</ref> On 18 August, ACT's spokesperson for children Chhour said the party would campaign on introducing a "Working with Children Card" for vetting people working with children and young people. This system would work separately from police checks.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT proposes 'Working with Children' card to ensure continual vetting |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1044039/act-proposes-working-with-children-card-to-ensure-continual-vetting |access-date=21 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=18 August 2026}}</ref>

On 21 August, Henry announced that ACT would campaign on building a "National Cancer Centre" with two campuses in [[Auckland]] and [[Christchurch]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ricketts |first1=Emma |title=Paul Henry gets his cancer centre in new ACT policy |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361023127/paul-henry-gets-his-cancer-centre-new-act-policy |access-date=24 August 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=21 August 2026}}</ref> On 23 August, Seymour confirmed that ACT would campaign on amending several laws including the [[New Zealand Bill of Rights Act 1990|Bill of Rights Act 1990]], the [[Constitution Act 1986]] and the Legislation Act 2019 to entrench the principles of equal rights, the rule of law and [[parliamentary sovereignty]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ricketts |first1=Emma |title='Are we engaging with Maori? ... that's your answer': ACT announces one law for all policy |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361023616/act-announces-one-law-all-policy |access-date=24 August 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=23 August 2026}}</ref> On 26 August, ACT announced that it would revive its 2023 election pledge to abolish the [[Climate Change Commission]].<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT Party revives policy to disestablish Climate Change Commission |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1131127/act-party-revives-policy-to-disestablish-climate-change-commission |access-date=30 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=26 August 2026}}</ref> On 30 August, ACT announces its fisheries policies, which included imposing a nationwide limit of ten shellfish per bag in order to replenish fishing stocks.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Natanahira |first1=Tuwhenuaroa |title=ACT announces plans for fisheries reform, would impose shellfish bag limits and increase fines|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1179393/act-announces-plans-for-fisheries-reform-would-impose-shellfish-bag-limits-and-increase-fines|access-date=5 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=30 August 2026}}</ref> On 31 August, the party said it would campaign on abolishing the permanent residency visa category and raising the physical presence requirements for resident visa holders.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Tupa'i |first1=Zeb |title=ACT proposes scrapping New Zealand's permanent resident visa|url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/act-proposes-scrapping-new-zealands-permanent-resident-visa/VH74HFLP75CLXGGWVKN4YBKHNI/ |access-date=5 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=1 September 2026}}</ref> 

On 4 September, ACT released its transport policy, which proposed excluding so-called "non-functional art" from the National Land Transport Fund.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McCulloch |first1=Craig |title=ACT promises to axe millions in roadside service spending|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1257242/act-promises-to-axe-millions-in-roadside-art-spending |access-date=5 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=4 September 2026}}</ref> On 6 September, the party released its climate change policy; which included eliminating New Zealand's net zero emissions target, carbon taxation, and repealing [[Climate Change Response (Zero Carbon) Amendment Act 2019]].<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT unveils election climate policy, vows to scrap net zero target and repeal Zero Carbon Act |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1272629/act-unveils-election-climate-policy-vows-to-scrap-net-zero-target-and-repeal-zero-carbon-act |access-date=9 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=6 September 2026}}</ref> On 10 September, ACT announced it would campaign on dissolving the [[Waitangi Tribunal]] once all outstanding treaty claims filed by 1 September 2008 had been resolved.<ref name="Smith Waitangi 10 Sep 2026">{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Sam |title=ACT would disestablish Waitangi Tribunal once outstanding Treaty claims completed |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361031471/act-would-disestablish-waitangi-tribunal-once-outstanding-treaty-claims-completed |access-date=10 September 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=10 September 2026}}</ref> On 13 September, ACT released its party list.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Natanahira |first1=Tuwhenuaroa |title=ACT unveils party list, Paul Henry could enter Parliament on current polling |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1356695/act-unveils-party-list-paul-henry-could-enter-parliament-on-current-polling |access-date=17 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=13 September 2026}}</ref> On 15 September, the party released its small business policies; which included capping the [[minimum wage]] for the next three years, introducing a lower "training wage" for workers under 20-years old, and tightening personal grievance rules.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT outlines policy to freeze adult minimum wage for three years |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/09/15/act-outlines-policy-to-freeze-adult-minimum-wage-for-three-years/ |access-date=17 September 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=15 September 2026}}</ref>

On 18 September, ACT's energy spokesperson [[Simon Court]] released the party's electrical policy, which included retaining the energy companies, using "market forces" to curb lines management costs and allowing residential solar power to be sold back to the power grid at market rates.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=ACT pledges to stop power companies being broken apart, open up solar market |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1455129/act-pledges-to-stop-power-companies-being-broken-apart-open-up-solar-market |access-date=20 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=18 September 2026}}</ref> On 26 September, ACT announced they would campaign on rolling out 20 [[noise camera]]s to combat boy racers in Auckland's [[Tāmaki]] suburb.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT promises noise cameras to combat boy racers in Tāmaki |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1587808/act-promises-noise-cameras-to-combat-boy-racers-in-tamaki |access-date=27 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=26 September 2026}}</ref> On 28 September, ACT released its supermarket policy, which proposed establishing a one-stop consenting shop for establishing new supermarkets and accepting food labels from overseas regulators by default. Seymour also ruled out endorsing the other parties' interventionist policies towards the country's [[Supermarkets in New Zealand|supermarket duopoly]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hu |first1=Justin |title=ACT reveals supermarkets policy, decries 'insane' break-up proposals |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/09/28/act-reveals-supermarkets-policy-decries-insane-break-up-proposals/ |access-date=28 September 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=28 September 2026}}</ref> On 30 September, Seymour said that the party would campaign on getting the Government to buy the [[University of Auckland]]'s former education campus in [[Epsom, New Zealand|Epsom]] for the purpose of establishing a new school.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Crimp |first1=Lauren |title=David Seymour launches campaign to buy land for new Epsom school |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1657425/david-seymour-launches-campaign-to-buy-land-for-new-epsom-school |access-date=3 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=30 September 2026}}</ref>

On 3 October, ACT pledged to ban resource consent conditions requiring people to participate or pay for religious or spiritual ceremonies such as [[karakia]] if elected.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT pledges to ban compulsory karakia from resource consents if elected |url=https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2026/10/03/act-pledges-to-ban-compulsory-karakia-from-resource-consents-if-elected/ |access-date=4 October 2026 |work=[[Te Ao Māori News]] |date=3 October 2026}}</ref> On 4 October, Seymour said that ACT would campaign on removing tax on [[KiwiSaver]] earnings in order to boost savings.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT pledges removing tax on KiwiSaver earnings |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1714907/act-pledges-removing-tax-on-kiwisaver-earnings |access-date=October 5, 2026 |newspaper=[[The Economist]] |date=October 4, 2026}}</ref> On 11 October, ACT released its fiscal plan, which included progressively raising the retirement age and reducing disability benefits by $653 million.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=ACT sets out plan to raise retirement age, cut debt and spending |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/10/11/act-sets-out-plan-to-raise-retirement-age-cut-debt-and-spending/ |access-date=12 October 2026 |work=[[1News]] |agency=[[RNZ]] |date=11 October 2026}}</ref> On 14 October, Seymour pledged to provide [[tax relief]] to landlords by allowing them to offset residential property losses against other taxable income.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT promises tax relief for landlords with offset rental losses |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1888418/act-promises-tax-relief-for-landlords-with-offset-rental-losses |access-date=16 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=14 October 2026}}</ref> On 18 October, ACT proposed investing $400 million to retain general practitioners in New Zealand and attract overseas doctors over a four-year period.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT wants funding boost to retain GPs, attract more from overseas |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1974584/act-wants-funding-boost-to-retain-gps-attract-more-from-overseas |access-date=18 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=18 October 2026}}</ref>

On 19 October, Paul Henry said that ACT would introduce an "industry awards" scheme as an alternative to the "University Entrance" scheme in order to encourage more high school graduates to enter trades training.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title='Stop railroading children': ACT backs trade training alternative to University Entrance |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2039807/stop-railroading-children-act-backs-trades-training-alternative-to-university-entrance |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=20 October 2026}}</ref> On 22 October, ACT announced plans to boost [[charter school]] enrollments by 10,000 by 2029 by establishing new charter schools in major centres and fast-tracking the conversion of state and state-integrated schools into charter ones.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT wants 10,000 charter school enrolments by 2029 |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2078887/act-wants-10-000-charter-school-enrolments-by-2029 |access-date=25 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=22 October 2026}}</ref>

On 26 October, ACT released its campaign manifesto, which proposed  cutting $22 billion in so-called "wasteful spending" over the next four years. The party also dropped its earlier pledge to scrap the Permanent Resident Visa Category in response to "anxiety" among migrant communities.<ref name="ACT manifesto 26 Oct 2026">{{Cite news |last1=McCulloch |first1=Craig |date=26 October 2026 |title=ACT publishes manifesto with $22 billion in cuts, ditches migration pledge|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2163416/act-publishes-manifesto-with-22b-in-cuts-ditches-migration-promise |access-date=26 October 2026|work=[[RNZ]] |language=en-nz}}</ref>

====New Zealand First====
Despite supporting the passage of the Government's [[Regulatory Standards Act 2025]] into law on 13 November 2025, New Zealand First announced on 20 November that it would repeal the legislation if re-elected into government in 2026. NZ First leader [[Winston Peters]] said that the party had only supported the Regulatory Standards Act due to [[ACT New Zealand]]'s coalition agreement with the National Party.<ref>{{cite news |last=Smith |first=Anneke |title=Winston Peters vows to repeal Regulatory Standards Bill; David Seymour hits back |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/579493/winston-peters-vows-to-repeal-regulatory-standards-bill-david-seymour-hits-back |access-date=23 November 2025 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=20 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251122073658/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/579493/winston-peters-vows-to-repeal-regulatory-standards-bill-david-seymour-hits-back |archive-date=22 November 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 10 February 2026, NZ First deputy leader [[Shane Jones]] confirmed the party would campaign on reinstating ministerial powers in approving [[Fast-track Approvals Act 2024|fast-track projects]] during the 2026 election.<ref>{{cite news |last=Smith |first=Anneke |title=NZ First to campaign on ministers getting final say in fast track projects |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/586414/nz-first-to-campaign-on-ministers-getting-final-say-in-fast-track-projects |access-date=12 February 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=10 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260210160325/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/586414/nz-first-to-campaign-on-ministers-getting-final-say-in-fast-track-projects |archive-date=10 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 12 February, the party confirmed it would campaign for a referendum on the future of the [[Māori electorates]].<ref>{{cite news |title=#election2026: New Zealand First to Campaign for Referendum on Māori Seats |url=https://waateanews.com/2026/02/12/election2026-new-zealand-first-to-campaign-for-referendum-on-maori-seats/ |access-date=12 February 2026 |work=[[Radio Waatea|Waatea News]] |date=12 February 2026}}</ref>

In his state of the nation address on 22 March 2026, Peters announced that NZ First would campaign on splitting the four energy companies. Former National MP and [[NewZeal]] leader [[Alfred Ngaro]] announced that he would be standing as an NZ First candidate.<ref>{{cite news |title=Winston Peters announces proposal to overhaul energy sector in State of the Nation speech |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/590317/winston-peters-announces-proposal-to-overhaul-energy-sector-in-state-of-the-nation-speech |access-date=22 March 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=22 March 2026 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260322223021/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/590317/winston-peters-announces-proposal-to-overhaul-energy-sector-in-state-of-the-nation-speech |archive-date=22 March 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Pearse |first=Adam |date=22 March 2026 |title=Winston Peters' State of the Nation: Former National minister Alfred Ngaro to stand for NZ First|url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/winston-peters-state-of-the-nation-new-candidate-and-energy-policy-expected-in-tauranga-address/FCZ3PZ23HRELDBZV5CC2RCVRS4/|access-date=22 March 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260322221828/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/winston-peters-state-of-the-nation-new-candidate-and-energy-policy-expected-in-tauranga-address/FCZ3PZ23HRELDBZV5CC2RCVRS4/ |archive-date=22 March 2026|url-status=live}}</ref> That same day, Ngaro confirmed that he and his party would align with NZ First, citing "strategic realism and shared values."<ref>{{cite web |title= Alfred Ngaro Announces Alignment with New Zealand First |url= https://newzeal.org.nz/press-releases/for-immediate-release-alfred-ngaro-announces-alignment-with-new-zealand-first |publisher=[[NewZeal]] |access-date=22 March 2026 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260322221432/https://newzeal.org.nz/press-releases/for-immediate-release-alfred-ngaro-announces-alignment-with-new-zealand-first |archive-date=22 March 2026 |date=22 March 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 27 March, Peters announced that NZ First would commit $15 million to rebuilding the [[Christchurch Cathedral]] if it was part of the next government.<ref>{{cite news |title=NZ First commits to $15 million towards Christ Church Cathedral rebuild if elected |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/590827/nz-first-commits-to-15-million-towards-christ-church-cathedral-rebuild-if-elected |access-date=28 March 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=27 March 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260327052229/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/590827/nz-first-commits-to-15-million-towards-christ-church-cathedral-rebuild-if-elected |archive-date=27 March 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 29 March, Peters said that the party would campaign on expanding mining operations and curbing the enforcement powers of the [[Department of Conservation (New Zealand)|Department of Conservation]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Expanding mining: NZ First want to declaw DOC, extend permits, return half of royalties to local regions |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/top/590955/expanding-mining-nz-first-want-to-declaw-doc-extend-permits-return-half-of-royalties-to-local-regions |access-date=30 March 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=29 March 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260330020853/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/top/590955/expanding-mining-nz-first-want-to-declaw-doc-extend-permits-return-half-of-royalties-to-local-regions |archive-date=30 March 2026}}</ref>

On 19 April, NZ First said it would campaign on breaking up [[Supermarkets in New Zealand|New Zealand's supermarket duopoly]] as a means of lowering food prices. The party has proposed splitting grocery company [[Foodstuffs (company)|Foodstuffs]] into two countrywide cooperatives—where one operates [[New World (supermarket)|New World]] and [[Four Square (supermarket)|Four Square]], and the other operates [[Pak'nSave]]—in order to compete with [[Woolworths (New Zealand)|Woolworths]].<ref>{{cite news |title=New Zealand First to campaign on breaking up supermarket duopoly |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/new-zealand-first-to-campaign-on-breaking-up-supermarket-duopoly/XFTP27CLQVC3RCFERUJZSME43E/ |access-date=19 April 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=19 April 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260419230449/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/new-zealand-first-to-campaign-on-breaking-up-supermarket-duopoly/XFTP27CLQVC3RCFERUJZSME43E/ |archive-date=19 April 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 17 May, Peters said that the party would campaign on nationalising the [[Bank of New Zealand]] and merging it with [[Kiwibank]] to form a proposed Crown-owned "National Bank of New Zealand." He also proposed enrolling all newborn citizens into a Crown-funded [[Kiwisaver]] programme.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gabel |first1=Julia |title=Election 2026: NZ First will campaign on buying back the BNZ bank and making KiwiSaver enrolment compulsory at birth |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/winston-peters-speaks-amid-polling-surge-as-nz-first-preps-for-election-fight/PFSTOI7CWFFSPH2RIUPNJPNAYY/ |access-date=17 May 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=17 May 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260517225754/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/winston-peters-speaks-amid-polling-surge-as-nz-first-preps-for-election-fight/PFSTOI7CWFFSPH2RIUPNJPNAYY/ |archive-date=17 May 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 24 May, Peters said NZ First would campaign on establishing a [[special economic zone]] at [[Marsden Point]] to provide relief from planning regulations and the [[Resource Management Act 1991]] in the area.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=NZ First wants Special Economic Zone at Marsden Point |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/regional/596186/nz-first-wants-special-economic-zone-at-marsden-point |access-date=25 May 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=24 May 2026}}</ref>

On 18 June, Peters said NZ First would campaign on disestablishing the [[Auckland Council]]'s [[Independent Māori Statutory Board]] as part of their platform against [[co-governance]].<ref name="Natanahira NZ First 18 Jun 2026">{{cite news |last=Natanahira |first= Tuwhenuaroa |title=New Zealand First to campaign on scrapping independent Māori Statutory Board |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/598582/new-zealand-first-to-campaign-on-scrapping-independent-maori-statutory-board |access-date=18 June 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=18 June 2026}}</ref> On 5 July, Peters said that the party would campaign on restricting voting rights to those holding [[New Zealand citizenship]]. Under the current law, certain legal residents including permanent residents are eligible to vote in elections.<ref>{{cite news |title=NZ First announces policy to restrict voting to citizens only |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/673830/nz-first-announces-policy-to-restrict-voting-to-citizens-only |access-date=5 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=5 July 2026}}</ref> During NZ First's campaign launch in Auckland on 19 July, Peters said that the party would campaign on investing in oil and gas exploration through a newly-established Future Fund. Peters ruled out working with Labour. Deputy leader Jones confirmed that he would contest the [[Northland (electorate)|Northland electorate]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Hanly |first=Lillian |title=NZ First promises to invest in oil, gas surveys |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/742372/nz-first-promises-to-invest-in-oil-gas-surveys |access-date=19 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=19 July 2026}}</ref> On 22 July 2026, [[Stuart Nash]] stood down as a NZ First candidate after being criticised for making sexist remarks about National MP [[Katie Nimon]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hanly |first=Lillian |date=23 July 2026 |title='Significant distraction': Stuart Nash resigns as NZ First candidate after 'sexist' comments |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/772673/significant-distraction-stuart-nash-resigns-as-nz-first-candidate-after-sexist-comments |access-date=22 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]]}}</ref>

On 2 August, NZ First released its [[natalist]] "Kiwi Kids Grant" policy, which proposed paying parents $5,000 a year for the first three years, which would cover a maximum of three children. The party also confirmed former parliamentarian and cabinet minister [[Ron Mark]] as one of its candidates.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=New Zealand First Kiwi Kids Grant plans to pay parents for having children |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/862035/new-zealand-first-kiwi-kids-grant-plans-to-pay-parents-for-having-children |access-date=3 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=3 August 2026}}</ref> On 6 September, the party announced it would campaign on opening [[Department of Conservation (New Zealand)|conservation land]] to economic development including mining.<ref>{{cite news |last1=O'Dwyer |first1=Ellen |title=NZ First to campaign for conservation land to be open for economic development |url=https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2026/09/06/nz-first-to-campaign-for-conservation-land-to-be-open-for-economic-development/ |access-date=9 September 2026 |work=[[Te Ao Māori News]] |agency=[[RNZ]] |date=6 September 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260907174223/https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2026/09/06/nz-first-to-campaign-for-conservation-land-to-be-open-for-economic-development/ |archive-date=7 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> That same day, Peters said that NZ First would campaign on restricting [[superannuation]] to [[New Zealand citizenship law|New Zealand citizens]].<ref>{{cite news |title=New Zealand First pledges to restrict Superannuation to citizens from 2029 |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1273306/new-zealand-first-pledges-to-restrict-superannuation-to-citizens-from-2029 |access-date=9 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=6 September 2026}}</ref> On 11 September, NZ First announced 14 immigration policies including tightening citizenship and visa requirements, strengthening powers to deport criminal non-citizens, demanding the immigration portfolio and holding a referendum on capping resident visas at 20,000.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=NZ First immigration policies revealed: Citizenship, deportation and resident visa cap referendum among policy platform |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/nz-first-immigration-policies-revealed-citizenship-deportation-and-resident-visa-cap-referendum-among-policy-platform/NNV7VPPRWFGJLKEYXJX6YCVV64/ |access-date=14 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=11 September 2026}}</ref>

On 22 September, Peters denied that NZ First had copied their health policy from an advocacy group called "Make New Zealand Healthy Again" but acknowledged that the party had been in contact with the group for several years.<ref>{{cite news|title='We wrote the policy' – Winston Peters denies copying campaign group's health policies|work=[[RNZ]]|access-date=26 September 2026|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1520353/we-wrote-the-policy-winston-peters-denies-copying-campaign-group-s-health-policies|date=22 September 2026}}</ref> On 26 September, NZ First announced it would campaign on dissolving the [[Waitangi Tribunal]] once all outstanding treaty claims filed by 1 September 2008 had been resolved.<ref name="1News Waitangi 26 Sep 2026">{{cite news|title=NZ First vow to 'immediately abolish' Waitangi Tribunal if elected|work=[[1News]]|access-date=26 September 2026|url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/09/26/nz-first-vow-to-immediately-abolish-waitangi-tribunal-if-elected/|date=26 September 2026}}</ref>

On 7 October, NZ First released its 25-member party list, which had several prominent newcomers including former parliamentarians [[Alfred Ngaro]], [[Michael Laws]], [[Harete Hipango]], former [[Financial Markets Authority (New Zealand)|Financial Markets Authority]] chair Craig Stobo and [[All Blacks]] rugby veteran [[Taine Randell]].<ref>{{cite news |title=New candidates hop sitting MPs as NZ First releases list |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/10/07/new-candidates-hop-sitting-mps-as-nz-first-releases-list/ |access-date=7 October 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=7 October 2026}}</ref> On 9 October, NZ First pledged to ban the public sale and private use of fireworks on animal welfare grounds.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McCulloch |first1=Craig |title='Enough is enough': New Zealand First wants to ban fireworks |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1801767/enough-is-enough-new-zealand-first-wants-to-ban-fireworks |access-date=9 October 2026 |work=[[New Zealand First]] |date=9 October 2026}}</ref> On 10 October, NZ First pledged that it would seek to permanently raise the Working for Families in-work tax credits to $70.<ref>{{cite news |title=NZ First vows to permanently raise Working for Family payments |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/10/10/nz-first-vows-to-permanently-raise-working-for-families-payments/ |access-date=12 October 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=10 October 2026}}</ref> On 11 October, Peters said the party would campaign on tightening work migration policies by abolishing the "Accredited Working Visa" in favour of a shortage-led Skilled Work Visa.<ref>{{cite news |title=NZ First promises to implement 'shortage-led' skilled work visas |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/10/11/nz-first-promises-to-implement-shortage-led-skilled-work-visas/ |access-date=12 October 2026 |work=[[1News]] |agency=[[RNZ]] |date=11 October 2026}}</ref>

On 14 October, NZ First pledged to amend the [[Fast-track Approvals Act 2024|fast-track approvals regime]] to ensure that future developments include planning for necessary roading, three waters, and other essential infrastructure. Prime Minister Luxon expressed disagreement with NZ First's pledge, stating that this policy had already been codified into the relevant legislation.<ref>{{cite news |title=NZ First wants to change fast-track regime, to save Kiwis 'suffering' through developments |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1887394/nz-first-wants-to-change-fast-track-regime-to-save-kiwis-suffering-through-developments |access-date=16 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=14 October 2026}}</ref> On 15 October, Peters pledged to increase the [[Royal New Zealand Coastguard]]'s [[New Zealand Crown|Crown funding]] from 40% to 70%.<ref>{{cite news |title=New Zealand First pledges to increase Coastguard funding |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1919618/new-zealand-first-pledges-to-increase-coastguard-funding |access-date=16 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=15 October 2026}}</ref> The same day, Peters said NZ First would campaign on overhauling combat sports regulations to improve safety and stop criminal influence.<ref>{{cite news |title=NZ First announces plan to overhaul combat sports regulations |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1924807/nz-first-announces-plan-to-overhaul-combat-sports-regulations |access-date=16 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=15 October 2026}}</ref> On 18 October, Peters said that NZ First would campaign on introducing a 12-month commuter train trial in the [[Canterbury Region]], as well as restoring the [[Southerner (New Zealand train)|Southerner]] train line between [[Christchurch]] and [[Invercargill]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title='The railway lines are already here': NZ First backs commuter rail trial in Canterbury |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1989296/the-railway-lines-are-already-here-nz-first-backs-commuter-rail-trial-in-canterbury |access-date=19 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=18 October 2026}}</ref>

On 20 October, NZ First proposed increasing government subsidies for video game developers from 20 percent to 40 percent in order to support the local video game industry.<ref>{{cite news |title=NZ First proposes big increase to taxpayer subsidies for video game developers |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2040312/nz-first-proposes-big-increase-to-taxpayer-subsidies-for-video-game-developers |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=20 October 2026}}</ref> On 22 October, Peters announced several law and order policies including introducing "life means life" provisions into [[life imprisonment|life sentences]], "[[stand your ground]]" self-defence legislation, and a formal "degree-of-murder" sentencing regime.<ref>{{cite news |title=Election 26: NZ First pledges tough sentences for serious crime |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2087703/election-26-nz-first-pledges-tough-sentences-for-serious-crime |access-date=25 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=22 October 2026}}</ref> On 27 October, Peters announced NZ First's $4 billion  "Commonsense Express" rail policy; with ten key promises including delivering the $1.135 billion Rail Network Investment Programme and the new [[Interislander]] ferries, making the [[Te Huia]] railway service permanent, and removing dangerous rail level crossings.<ref>{{cite news |title=NZ First announces $4b 'Commonsense Express' plan for rail |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2193460/nz-first-announces-4b-commonsense-express-plan-for-rail |access-date=28 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=27 October 2026}}</ref>

====Te Pāti Māori====
In February 2025, Te Pāti Māori proposed the creation of a Parliamentary Commissioner for [[Treaty of Waitangi|Te Tiriti o Waitangi]], who would have extraordinary powers to audit and veto bills that did not comply with the Treaty. The party described the policy as a "bottomline" in any coalition negotiations.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McConnell |first1=Glenn |title=Te Pāti Māori issues coalition ultimatum, naming Treaty Commissioner as a 'bottom line' |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360570421/te-pati-maori-issues-coalition-ultimatum-naming-treaty-commissioner-bottom-line |access-date=13 February 2025 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=4 February 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250213215804/https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360570421/te-pati-maori-issues-coalition-ultimatum-naming-treaty-commissioner-bottom-line |archive-date=13 February 2025}}</ref> In April 2025, Te Pāti Māori announced plans to run candidates in the general seats at the 2026 general election.<ref>{{cite news |title=Te Pāti Māori to run candidates in general seats at next election |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/557788/te-pati-maori-to-run-candidates-in-general-seats-at-next-election |access-date=10 April 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=10 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250410234523/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/557788/te-pati-maori-to-run-candidates-in-general-seats-at-next-election |archive-date=10 April 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

In January 2026, the party stated they will campaign on [[prison abolition|abolishing prisons]] by 2040 to address the high Māori incarceration rate, replacing them with "community-led and community-based solutions".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gabel |first1=Julia |title=Election 2026: Te Pāti Māori to campaign on abolishing prisons, Labour dead-set against it |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-te-pati-maori-to-campaign-on-abolishing-prisons-labour-dead-set-against-it/25RU6JTA7JDVVDHLV6AS7ONMNQ/ |access-date=26 January 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=26 January 2026}}</ref> On 27 May, Te Pāti Māori announced  Ngātiwai Trust Board chairman Aperahama Edwards as its candidate for [[Te Tai Tokerau]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Crimp |first1=Lauren |title=Aperahama Edwards to run for Te Pāti Māori in Te Tai Tokerau |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/596458/aperahama-edwards-to-run-for-te-pati-maori-in-te-tai-tokerau |access-date=26 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=27 May 2026}}</ref> On 23 June, the party's co-leaders [[Debbie Ngarewa-Packer]] and [[Rawiri Waititi]] announced that Te Pāti Māori's electoral strategy was to encourage Māori voters to register for the [[Māori electorates|Māori roll]]. Waititi said that they would encourage Māori voters to give their electorate vote to Te Pāti Māori and their party vote to either Labour or the Greens.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Natanahira |first1=Tuwhenuaroa |title=Te Pāti Māori lay out their strategy to defeat the coalition |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/617536/te-pati-maori-lay-out-their-strategy-to-defeat-the-coalition |access-date=26 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=23 July 2026}}</ref> 

Te Pāti Māori launched its electoral campaign on 16 August and introduced its [[Ikaroa-Rāwhiti]] candidate Haley Maxwell.<ref name="Natanahira 16 Aug 2026">{{cite news |last1=Natanahira |first1=Tuwhenuaroa |title=Te Pāti Māori reveals election strategy as campaign launches in Gisborne |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1011909/te-pati-maori-reveals-election-strategy-as-campaign-launches-in-gisborne |access-date=17 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=16 August 2026}}</ref> On 26 August, the party launched its "Kiwi Tax Plan" with key tax policies including a 48% tax rate on those earning NZ$300,001 and above, a rebate for New Zealanders earning less than NZ$30,000, a wealth tax, an increased company tax rate, a land banking tax, a vacant house tax and a stamp duty.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ricketts |first1=Emma |title=Te Pāti Māori proposes 48% income tax rate, tax credit for kai |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361025236/te-pati-maori-proposes-48-income-tax-rate-tax-credit-kai |access-date=30 August 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=26 August 2026}}</ref> 

On 1 September, the party announced several policies aimed at constitutional reform including establishing an independent "Te Tiriti Commission" to investigate alleged breaches of the [[Treaty of Waitangi]] by the [[New Zealand Crown]], making [[Waitangi Tribunal]] recommendations legally binding, establishing a 2040 target for constitutional reform and establishing a $200 million Matike Mai Fund to implement constitutional reform and the [[United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples]] (UNDRIP).<ref name="Natanahira 1 September 2026">{{cite news |last1=Natanahira |first1=Tuwhenuaroa |title=Te Pāti Māori vows major constitutional reform by making Te Tiriti binding for Crown|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1207353/te-pati-maori-vows-major-constitutional-reform-by-making-te-tiriti-binding-for-crown|access-date=7 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=1 September 2026}}</ref> On 16 September, Te Pāti Māori announced it would create a $1 billion fund to increase Māori and community ownership of new renewable energy assets.<ref>{{cite news |title=Te Pāti Māori announces $1 billion fund for 'energy sovereignty' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1411215/te-pati-maori-announces-1-billion-fund-for-energy-sovereignty |access-date=17 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=16 September 2026}}</ref> On 21 September, the party released its "kai (food) sovereignty" policy; which included allowing Māori collectives to secure Crown and council-owned land, allocating $100 million in [[seed funding]] to establish supermarkets, and an income tax food credit policy.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=Election 2026: Te Pāti Māori to give Māori collectives Crown-owned land, $100m to set up supermarket chains |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-te-pati-maori-to-give-maori-collectives-crown-owned-land-100m-to-set-up-supermarket-chains/5IAESMBZ5BEN7GWWD2RU36JZCI/ |access-date=27 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=21 September 2026}}</ref> On 23 September, Te Pāti Māori released its [[Takatāpui]] ([[LGBTQ]]) policy which involved setting up a $200 million Takatāpui Commission with powers and funding to uplift children.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Natanahira |first1=Tuwhenuaroa |title=Te Pāti Māori promises $200 million to change 'who holds power' for decisions about takatāpui |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1530281/te-pati-maori-promises-200-million-to-change-who-holds-power-for-decisions-about-takatapui |access-date=27 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=23 September 2026}}</ref>

On 4 October, Te Pāti Māori released its party list, which consisted of its Secretary Lance Norman. The party is standing seven candidates in the seven [[Māori electorates]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ricketts |first1=Emma |title=Te Pati Maori reinforces strategic voting call with sparse party list |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361042156/te-pati-maori-reinforces-strategic-voting-call-sparse-party-list |access-date=7 October 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=4 October 2026}}</ref>

====Opportunity====
The [[Opportunity Party (New Zealand)|Opportunity Party]] (TOP) has announced it would campaign with several policies, including reducing house prices through a land value tax, and introducing a "Citizen's Voice", consisting of [[Citizens' assembly|citizens' assemblies]] for certain major issues.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Platform |url=https://www.opportunity.org.nz/platform |access-date=14 February 2026|website=Opportunity Party |language=en}}</ref> In mid-February 2026, party leader [[Qiulae Wong]] announced that Opportunity would campaign on replacing all forms of welfare assistance including [[New Zealand Superannuation Fund|superannuation]] with a means-tested "citizen's income." Unlike [[universal basic income]], people earning more than NZ$350,000 a year would not be able to access it. The citizen's income policy would be funded by a land value tax, and savings from the benefits that would be replaced.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Edmunds |first1=Susan |title=Ditch NZ Super entirely, minor party says |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/586825/ditch-nz-super-entirely-minor-party-says |access-date=14 February 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=13 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260213065627/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/586825/ditch-nz-super-entirely-minor-party-says |archive-date=13 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 16 February, the party's general manager [[Iain Lees-Galloway]] announced that the party would field about 30 candidates during the 2026 election with the goal of reaching the five percent threshold for the party needed to enter Parliament.<ref name="Schmidt 16 Feb 2026">{{cite web |last1=Schmidt |first1=Veronica |title=Iain Lees-Galloway on crashing out of parliament and his new Opportunity |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/16-02-2026/iain-lees-galloway-on-crashing-out-of-parliament-and-his-new-opportunity |website=[[The Spinoff]] |access-date=16 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260216072029/https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/16-02-2026/iain-lees-galloway-on-crashing-out-of-parliament-and-his-new-opportunity |archive-date=16 February 2026 |date=16 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> During the party's State of the Nation address in Auckland on 21 February, Wong announced that the Opportunity Party would campaign on promoting national unity, banning [[bottom trawling]] and investing in renewal energy.<ref>{{cite web |author1=Opportunity Party |title='We Are The Next Generation Of Leaders' Says Qiulae Wong |url=https://m.scoop.co.nz/stories/PO2602/S00118/we-are-the-next-generation-of-leaders-says-qiulae-wong.htm |website=[[Scoop (website)|Scoop]] |access-date=22 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260221035522/https://m.scoop.co.nz/stories/PO2602/S00118/we-are-the-next-generation-of-leaders-says-qiulae-wong.htm |archive-date=21 February 2026 |date=21 February 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 20 March, the Opportunity Party released its initial list of 26 candidates.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Matich |first1=Troy |title=Election 2026: The Opportunity Party reveals candidates, promises more on the way |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-the-opportunity-party-reveals-candidates-promises-more-on-the-way/BDOG4A4YNZB5BPGEZB7V44XNJQ/ |access-date=21 March 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |agency=[[Newstalk ZB]] |date=20 March 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260321104106/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-the-opportunity-party-reveals-candidates-promises-more-on-the-way/BDOG4A4YNZB5BPGEZB7V44XNJQ/ |archive-date=21 March 2026}}</ref> 

On 2 July, Opportunity released its economic policy, which included investing in technology and commerce, strengthening the [[Commerce Commission]]'s powers and forgiving [[student loan]] interest in order to draw back emigrant New Zealanders.<ref>{{cite news |title=TOP unveils new economic policy |url=https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/on-air/heather-du-plessis-allan-drive/audio/qiulae-wong-top-leader-on-the-party-announcing-new-economic-policy/ |access-date=5 July 2026 |work=[[Newstalk ZB]] |date=2 July 2026}}</ref> On 31 July, the party released its party list of 40 electorate candidates.<ref>{{cite news |title=Opportunity Party reveals candidates for 40 electorates |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/850090/opportunity-party-reveals-candidates-for-40-electorates |access-date=30 July 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=31 July 2026}}</ref> On 16 August, Opportunity proposed several policies to regulate lobbying and political donations including capping political donations and establishing an independent anti-corruption entity to coordinate anti-corruption activities.<ref>{{cite news |title=Opportunity Party vows to 'clean up politics' with lobbying and donations reforms |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1010243/opportunity-party-vows-to-clean-up-politics-with-lobbying-and-donations-reforms |access-date=17 August 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=16 August 2026}}</ref>

On 18 September, Opportunity released its climate change policy, which included introducing a [[methane]] emissions trading scheme and investing $5 billion in [[climate adaptation]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Newton |first1=Kate |title=Opportunity proposes methane trading scheme, $5b climate adaptation plan |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/environment_climate/1453264/opportunity-proposes-methane-trading-scheme-5b-climate-adaptation-plan |access-date=19 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=18 September 2026}}</ref> On 30 September, Wong outlined the party's five "non-negotiable" policies including a ten-year health plan, lobbying reform, banning [[bottom trawling]], attacking [[monopolies]] and [[duopolies]] and bolstering the country's climate change commitments.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Meyer |first1=Fox |title=Opportunity's line in the sand slants to the left |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2026/09/30/opportunitys-line-in-the-sand-slants-to-the-left/ |access-date=3 October 2026 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=30 September 2026}}</ref>

=== Other parties and groups ===
On 7 June, the [[Maritime Union of New Zealand]]'s (MUNZ) national council unanimously voted to affiliate with the [[Alliance (New Zealand political party)|Alliance]] party. The union also confirmed it would continue its long-standing affiliation with the Labour Party.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/597493/maritime-union-affiliates-with-alliance-party-keeps-door-open-to-labour |title=Maritime Union affiliates with Alliance Party, keeps door open to Labour |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=7 June 2026 |access-date=9 June 2026 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260609145759/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/597493/maritime-union-affiliates-with-alliance-party-keeps-door-open-to-labour |archive-date=9 June 2026|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 20 October, Aaron Hendry of the youth development organisation "Kick Back" called on political leaders to commit to ending youth homelessness. Kick Back released a 12 calls for action to several political parties; with key planks including reversing the Government's restrictions on emergency housing, stopping benefit sanctions and "move on" orders, increasing public housing and restoring welfare benefits for 18 and 19 year olds. By 20 October the Green, Māori and Opportunity parties had accepted the 12 calls to action while Labour had accepted seven of the proposals. The National, ACT and NZ First parties declined to respond to these proposals.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Williams |first1=Amy |title=Political leaders called on to commit to ending youth homelessness |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2036532/political-leaders-called-on-to-commit-to-ending-youth-homelessness |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=20 October 2026}}</ref>

On 28 October, [[Local Government New Zealand]], [[Hurunui District Council]] and [[Environment Canterbury]] called on the next government to invest more funding into flood mitigation and climate resilience, citing the adverse financial and social impact of previous disasters including the [[2016 Kaikōura earthquake]] and the July 2026 [[Hurunui District|Hurunui]] flood. According to figures released by the [[Climate Change Commission]], the incumbent government had allocated 97 percent of government spending to responding to natural hazards, with only 3 percent being allocated to building resilience.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hill |first1=David |title=Councils call for more flood, climate resilience funding from next government |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/regions_local-democracy-reporting/2199107/councils-call-for-more-flood-climate-resilience-funding-from-next-government |access-date=28 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=28 October 2026}}</ref> The following day, [[Mayor of Auckland]] [[Wayne Brown (New Zealand politician)|Wayne Brown]] released a manifesto focusing on five priorities including improving public transportation, supporting growth in "the right places," supporting Auckland's economic drive, cutting "stupid rules" and  not neglecting the environment and resilience. Brown also criticised the central government for interfering with Auckland's roading and housing unitary policies, telling them to "sod off."<ref>{{cite news |title=Auckland mayor Wayne Brown's election plea: 'Sod off' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/2213698/auckland-mayor-wayne-brown-s-election-plea-sod-off |access-date=28 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=29 October 2026}}</ref>

=== Debates ===
The Opportunity Party qualified for TVNZ's planned multi-party debate with the 1 News–Verian poll in June, making it the only extra-parliamentary party to be invited.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/media-insider/media-insider-tv-showdown-the-race-to-host-political-debates-westpac-pauses-new-brand-campaign-will-traitors-nz-return-mike-hosking-on-zbs-100th-birthday/premium/C2VTNPQTANFU7APH425EBEMDW4/ |title=Media Insider: TV showdown – the race to host political debates; Westpac pauses new brand campaign; Will Traitors NZ return?; Mike Hosking on ZB's 100th birthday |date=7 August 2026 |work=New Zealand Herald |first=Shayne |last=Currie}}</ref> TVNZ, [[Stuff (company)|Stuff Group]], and the ''New Zealand Herald'' announced their debate schedules on 20 August 2026.<ref name="stuffdeb">{{cite news |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361022518/two-leaders-debates-interactive-multichannel-election-night-special-stuff-group-launches-aotearoa |title=Two Leaders' debates; interactive multichannel election night special – Stuff Group launches 'Aotearoa Decides' |date=20 August 2026 |work=Stuff}}</ref><ref name="nzhdeb">{{cite news |url=http://nzme.co.nz/news/nzme-unveils-its-biggest-ever-election-coverage-for-2026 |title=NZME unveils its biggest-ever election coverage for 2026 |date=20 August 2026 |work=NZME}}</ref> [[Christopher Luxon]] declined TVNZ's second proposed leaders debate.<ref name="tvnzdeb">{{cite news |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/08/20/luxon-vs-hipkins-tvnz-confirms-live-election-leaders-debate/ |title=Luxon vs Hipkins: TVNZ confirms live election Leaders Debate |date=20 August 2026 |work=1News}}</ref> 

In September 2026, the Green and Labour parties pulled out of a series of local electorate-based debates hosted by the [[New Zealand Taxpayers' Union]] following allegations made by [[Nicky Hager]] in his book ''[[Dirty Work (Hager book)|Dirty Work]]''.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cooke |first1=Henry |url=https://www.thepost.co.nz/politics/361086090/green-party-pull-out-taxpayers-union-debates-following-nicky-hager-book |title=Labour, Greens pull out of Taxpayers' Union debates following Nicky Hager book |date=23 September 2026 |work=[[The Post (New Zealand newspaper)|The Post]] }}</ref> In October 2026, National candidate Karuna Muthu left in the middle of a [[Wellington Bays (electorate)|Wellington Bays]] candidates meeting after clashing with organisers for asking what he regarded as "loaded left-wing" questions.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Manera |first1=Ethan |title=Election 2026: National Party candidate Karuna Muthu storms out of Wellington Bays candidates meeting |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-national-party-candidate-karuna-muthu-storms-out-of-wellington-bays-candidates-meeting/2BPUSTZ5JVHFJL33BZ2AH6YNUY/ |access-date=18 October 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=17 October 2026}}</ref>

