==Issues==
===Public opinion polling===
According to an [[Ipsos]] Issues Monitor survey released in late May 2026, the top ten issues concerning voters during the 2026 general election were the inflation/[[Cost-of-living crisis#New Zealand|cost of living]] (61%), [[Healthcare in New Zealand|healthcare]] (39%), [[Economy of New Zealand#Post COVID-19 pandemic (2023–present)|the economy]] (33%), [[2026 fuel crisis#New Zealand|petrol prices]] (26%) and [[Housing in New Zealand#Housing affordability|housing]] (21%), [[Economy of New Zealand|unemployment]] (19%), poverty/[[Economic inequality in New Zealand|inequality]] (17%), [[Crime in New Zealand|crime]] (16%), [[Climate change in New Zealand|climate change]], and [[Education in New Zealand|education]]. Respondents ranked Labour as the political party most capable of managing inflation, healthcare, petrol prices, housing, unemployment, poverty and education. Respondents ranked National as most capable of handling the economy and crime while the Greens were ranked as most capable of handling climate change. Ipsos surveyed 1,001 New Zealanders aged 18 and over between 15 and 20 May. Results were weighted by age, gender and region, with a maximum margin of error of ±3.1% at a 95 percent confidence level.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=Fuel prices become fourth-biggest issue for New Zealanders, poll finds |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/596305/fuel-prices-become-fourth-biggest-issue-for-new-zealanders-poll-finds |access-date=27 May 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=26 May 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260525210121/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/596305/fuel-prices-become-fourth-biggest-issue-for-new-zealanders-poll-finds |archive-date=25 May 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

A follow-up Ipsos survey released in late September 2026 identified cost of living, healthcare, the economy, unemployment and housing as the top five issues for the 2026 general election. Respondents ranked Labour as the political party most capable of managing poverty/inequality, fuel, education, immigration, taxation, drug/alcohol abuse, debt, transportation/infrastructure, race relations and population/overpopulation. Respondents ranked National as most capable of handling law and order issues, defence, foreign affairs and terrorism. The Greens were ranked as most capable of handling climate change, environmental pollution and water concerns while Te Pāti Māori was considered the party best capable of managing Māori issues. The survey also found that 64% of respondents supported the Greens' proposed [[wealth tax]] of 2.5% on assets worth above $10 million (excluding family homes). Half of respondents also supported a tax on assets and gifts exceeding $1 million (excluding family homes and farms) along with a [[bed tax]] on short-term accommodation stays. The survey also found that 45% of respondents supported Labour's proposed [[capital gains tax]] while less than 25% of respondents supported the Opportunity Party's proposed [[land tax]]. Ipsos surveyed 1,003 New Zealanders aged 18 and over between 3 and 11 September, with a credibility interval of +/-3.5 percentage points.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Crimp |first1=Lauren |title=Labour's lead on top issues narrows as National gains – survey |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1563852/labour-s-lead-on-top-issues-narrows-as-national-gains-survey |access-date=29 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=25 September 2026}}</ref>

In late September 2026, a [[Gallup, Inc.|Gallup]] survey of 1,000 New Zealanders asking the question "what people need leaders to hear in 2026" found that key concerns were the economy (29.4%), social issues (19.9%), healthcare (9.5%), food and housing at (8.6%), politics and government (7.4%), and the environment (5.2%). Lesser issues of concern included crime and public safety (3.4%), infrastructure (1.2%), the media (1%) and immigration and refugees (0.6%). Analysing the results, [[University of Otago]] economist Associate Professor Dennis Wesselbaum suggested that New Zealanders were not solely concerned with the economy but were also concerned about affordability, social cohesion, healthcare and access to basic services.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Wesselbaum |first1=Dennis |title=What do NZ voters really care about? A global comparison reveals some surprises |url=https://theconversation.com/what-do-nz-voters-really-care-about-a-global-comparison-reveals-some-surprises-292760 |access-date=29 September 2026 |website=[[The Conversation (website)|The Conversation]] |date=28 September 2026}}</ref>

===Citizenship and immigration===
On 20 August 2026, a Curia Market Research poll conducted for the [[New Zealand Taxpayers' Union]] found that 59% of respondents favoured limiting voting in general elections to [[New Zealand nationality law|New Zealand citizens]], while 32% opposed excluding non-citizens and 9% were unsure. This poll was in response to the populist New Zealand First party campaigning in July 2026 to limit voting rights to citizens and excluding resident visa holders. The survey found majority support for restricting suffrage to citizens among supporters of the National, New Zealand First, ACT, Labour and Green parties while only 38% of Te Pāti Māori voters supported such a policy. Younger voters aged between 18 and 39s years (63%) were more likely than older age groups to support restricting voting to citizens; compared with 58% of voters aged between 40 and 59 years and 56% of voters aged 60 years and above. Curia's poll surveyed 1,000 people between 1 and 4 August.<ref>{{cite news |title=Poll: More Kiwis support restricting voting to citizens than oppose it – across the political spectrum |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361022465/poll-majority-kiwis-support-limiting-general-election-voting-citizens |access-date=21 August 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=20 August 2026}}</ref>

During the annual Immigration Conference in Auckland on 11 August, several politicians gave speeches outlining their parties' immigration policies. While ACT and NZ First have campaigned on tightening immigration controls, National, Labour and the Greens refrained from adopting [[populist]] [[anti-immigration]] policies. ACT has proposed replacing the ballot system for parent resident visas with a queue system and abolishing the permanent resident category visa. NZ First announced a dozen proposals to tighten migration and citizenship policies. National has  said it would retain the permanent resident category visa while opposing NZ First and the Greens' proposed amnesty for [[Pasifika New Zealanders|Pasifika]] overstayers. Labour has proposed creating a citizenship pathway for the children of overstayers, a longer permanent residency visa process, and reviewing the financial criteria for parental reunification visas. The Greens announced several policies including a "settled residency pathway" for migrants who have resided in New Zealand for five years, ending a 12-month stand-down period requiring workers to leave the countries while applying for visas, and allow migrant workers to change jobs.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell|title=ACT and NZ First pitch tighter immigration controls, others wash against immigration policies |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1340334/act-and-nz-first-pitch-tighter-immigration-controls-others-warn-against-anti-immigration-policies |access-date=14 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=11 September 2026}}</ref>

In late October 2026, ACT scrapped its campaign pledge to scrap the Permanent Resident Visa Category in response to "anxiety" among migrant communities.<ref name="ACT manifesto 26 Oct 2026" />

===Law and order===
On 14 October 2026, several Green and Te Pāti Māori candidates including Members of Parliament [[Oriini Kaipara]], [[Hana-Rawhiti Maipi-Clarke]], [[Hūhana Lyndon]] met with members of the criminal gang [[Mongrel Mob]] including [[Harry Tam]] during an election meeting near [[South Auckland]]. Photos of the meeting were circulated on social media. The meeting was criticised by National Party MP [[Paul Goldsmith (politician)|Paul Goldsmith]], who accused the Greens of "prioritising" gangs over victims, and victim's advocate Claire Buckley.<ref name="Dexter 14 Oct 2026">{{cite news |last1=Dexter |first1=Giles |title=Greens 'unapologetic' at Mongrel Mob meeting as National cries foul |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1896798/greens-unapologetic-at-mongrel-mob-meeting-as-national-cries-foul |access-date=18 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=14 October 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title='Furious': Advocate voices concerns after Greens and Te Pāti Māori reportedly meet with Mongrel Mob |url= https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/on-air/heather-du-plessis-allan-drive/audio/claire-buckley-independent-victims-advocate-on-te-p%C4%81ti-m%C4%81ori-and-green-mps-reportedly-meeting-with-mongrel-mob/ |access-date=18 October 2026 |work=[[Newstalk ZB]] |date=14 October 2026}}</ref> Similar criticism was voiced by Labour Party leader [[Chris Hipkins]], who ruled out appointing Green and Te Pāti Māori ministers to the police and corrections portfolios in a future-led Labour government. Labour's justice spokesperson [[Willie Jackson (politician)|Willie Jackson]] described the meeting as "not a good strategic move."<ref name="RNZ gang visit 15 Oct 2026">{{cite news |title=Greens' Marama Davidson doubles down amid outrage over gang visit |url= https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1923070/greens-marama-davidson-doubles-down-amid-outrage-over-gang-visit |access-date=18 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=15 October 2026}}</ref> In response to criticism, Greens co-leader [[Marama Davidson]] defended her party's engagement with what she called "marginalised communities" and said that the Greens also met with victims of crime.<ref name="Dexter 14 Oct 2026" /> The following day, Davidson doubled down on her position, stating that "ministers and MPs visit prisons and meet with criminals all the time to better understand what's going on."<ref name="RNZ gang visit 15 Oct 2026" /> Similar sentiments were echoed by Kaipara, who said their visit was not intended to condone gang activity but instead "meant recognising that whānau do not stop being whānau because of a patch."<ref name="RNZ gang visit 15 Oct 2026" /> Te Pāti Māori's general manager Kiri Tamihere-Waititi accused National and Labour of "manufacturing outrage."<ref>{{cite news |title=Election 2026: Kiri Tamihere-Waititi calls backlash to Mongrel Mob hui 'manufactured outrage' |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-kiri-tamihere-waititi-calls-backlash-to-mongrel-mob-hui-manufactured-outrage/3OQJVNY325HWBLJLMHSPXXMHRU/ |access-date=19 October 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=17 October 2026}}</ref>

[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]]'s associate editor Sam Sachdeva opined that the Greens' outreach to gang members could jeopardise gains that the party had made by campaigning on cost of living and economic issues.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sachdeva |first1=Sam |title=Greens' gains in jeopardy after Mongrel Mob stand |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2026/10/16/greens-gains-in-jeopardy-after-mongrel-mob-stand/ |access-date=18 October 2026 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=16 October 2026}}</ref> ''[[The Spinoff]]''{{'s}} Lyric Waiwiri-Smith described the politicians' photoshoot with gang members as a "spectacularly risky political move" while acknowledging that gang members were also constituents.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Wairiri-Smith |first1=Lyric |title=When gangs are in the picture, all you get are pot shots |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/16-10-2026/when-gangs-are-in-the-picture-all-you-get-are-pot-shots |website=[[The Spinoff]] |access-date=19 October 2026 |date=16 October 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261017064207/https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/16-10-2026/when-gangs-are-in-the-picture-all-you-get-are-pot-shots|archive-date=17 October 2026|url-status=live}}</ref> Left-wing blogger [[Martyn "Bomber" Bradbury]] defended the right of political leaders to meet with gang members to discuss social policy but was critical of their decision to pose for photos with gang members since it handed ammunition to their political enemies.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Bradbury |first1=Martyn |title=Heather Duplicity Allan, the curse of Metiria Turei and where was Oriini's Campaign Manager? |url=https://thedailyblog.co.nz/heather-duplicity-allan-the-curse-of-metiria-turei-and-where-was-oriinis-campaign-manager/ |website=The Daily Blog |access-date=19 October 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261019055236/https://thedailyblog.co.nz/heather-duplicity-allan-the-curse-of-metiria-turei-and-where-was-oriinis-campaign-manager/ |archive-date=19 October 2026 |date=18 October 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

After [[Te Ao Māori News]] released footage of Labour candidate [[Kerrin Leoni]] campaigning at a Mongrel Mob event in early October 2026,<ref>{{cite news |title=Video shows Labour candidate Kerrin Leoni at Mongrel Mob 'whānau' event |url=https://www.teaonews.co.nz/2026/10/22/video-shows-labour-candidate-kerrin-leoni-at-mongrel-mob-event/ |access-date=22 October 2026 |work=[[Te Ao Māori News]] |date=22 October 2026}}</ref> she resigned as a Labour candidate on 23 October. While Labour leader Hipkins had initially supported Leoni, he subsequently stated that footage of her campaigning alongside gangs was "inconsistent with the standards" expected for Labour candidates.  Since nominations for the 2026 general election had closed, Leoni's name will remain on the ballot but without Labour's endorsement.<ref>{{cite news |title=Election 2026 live updates: Kerrin Leoni resigns as Labour candidate after campaigning with Mongrel Mob |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2026-live-updates-kerrin-leoni-resigns-as-labour-candidate-after-campaigning-with-mongrel-mob/R5IJCEB4TNF3LETYQ5JOSBPH7A/ |access-date=22 October 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=23 October 2026}}</ref>

On 24 October, ''[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]]'' reported that National MP [[Tama Potaka]] had posed for a photograph with [[gang patch|patched]] members of the Mongrel Mob during [[Māori King movement|Māori King]] [[Tūheitia Paki]]'s Hui ā-motu event in January 2024. This photograph was released after National had criticised the Green, Māori and Labour parties for allegedly consorting with gang members and being "soft on crime." In response, Potaka told ''Stuff'' that one of the individuals was a former school mate and that they were attending a public event prior to the passage of the [[Gangs Act 2024|gang patch ban]]. Luxon and Police Minister [[Mark Mitchell (New Zealand politician)|Mark Mitchell]] defended Potaka, saying that he was not campaigning or fundraising among gang members during the 2024 hui.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lynch |first1=Jenna |title=Picture emerges of National minister posing with gang members |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361050603/picture-emerges-national-minister-posing-gang-members |access-date=24 October 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=24 October 2026}}</ref>

On 25 October, National removed a volunteer from his position as a member of their candidate Ankit Bansal's [[Palmerston North (electorate)|Palmerston North electorate]] committee for failing to disclose a prior sexual misconduct conviction.<ref>{{cite news |title=National Party electorate volunteer dismissed after sex conviction with minor revealed |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/nz-news/361050933/national-party-electorate-volunteer-dismissed-after-sex-conviction-minor-revealed |access-date=26 October 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=25 October 2026}}</ref>

===Māori issues===
[[New Zealand First]] campaigning for a referendum on the future of the [[Māori electorates]],<ref>{{cite news |title=#election2026: New Zealand First to Campaign for Referendum on Māori Seats |url=https://waateanews.com/2026/02/12/election2026-new-zealand-first-to-campaign-for-referendum-on-maori-seats/ |access-date=12 February 2026 |work=[[Radio Waatea|Waatea News]] |date=12 February 2026}}</ref> on disestablishing the [[Auckland Council]]'s [[Independent Māori Statutory Board]] as part of their platform against [[co-governance]].<ref name="Natanahira NZ First 18 Jun 2026" /> NZ First has also announced it would campaign on dissolving the [[Waitangi Tribunal]].<ref name="1News Waitangi 26 Sep 2026" /> ACT has also announced it would campaign on dissolving the [[Waitangi Tribunal]] once all outstanding treaty claims filed by 1 September 2008 had been resolved.<ref name="Smith Waitangi 10 Sep 2026" /> Conversely, Te Pāti Māori confirmed they would  campaign on creating a Te Tiriti Commission to investigate Crown breaches of the Treaty of Waitangi, and make the Waitangi Tribunal's recommendations binding.<ref name="Natanahira 1 September 2026" />

===Misinformation and disinformation===
On 9 September, the [[Advertising Standards Authority (New Zealand)|Advertising Standards Authority]] (ASA) partially upheld a complaint against a National Party attack ad alleging that the Labour Party's [[capital gains tax]] would hurt small businesses and [[superannuation]]. Labour has said that its proposed capital gains tax would not target superannuation, the family home, inheritances, shares, and small businesses. A majority of the ASA complaints board found that the National Party's ad claim that the capital gains tax would target GST was misleading. In response, the National Party's campaign chair Simeon Brown defended the ad, saying that the capital gains tax would have a flow-on effect on superannuation funds invested in businesses that owned property.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dexter  |first1=Giles |title=ASA partially upholds complaint against misleading National attack ad|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1311337/asa-partly-upholds-complaint-against-misleading-national-attack-ad |access-date=14 September 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=9 September 2026}}</ref>

On 22 September, Labour criticised National for publishing a social media campaign video attacking the former for being "soft on crime" while showing clips of violent crime footage from the early part of the [[54th New Zealand Parliament|National-led government's first term]]. In response, National's campaign chair [[Simeon Brown]] said that the video footage came from before the Government had passed its "tough on crime" legislation including [[Gangs Act 2024|banning gang patches]] and giving judges less discretion for sentencing. Labour's campaign chair [[Kieran McAnulty]] responded by accusing National of running a "dirty" election campaign.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=National attack ads against Labour use crime footage from government's own term |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1517678/national-attack-ads-against-labour-use-crime-footage-from-government-s-own-term |access-date=23 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=22 September 2026}}</ref>

In late October 2025, [[Victoria University of Wellington]] political scientist Mona Krewel's "New Zealand Social Media Study" found that the 2026 general election recorded the lowest level of disinformation on [[Facebook]] since the study began in 2020. Her study found 17 "half-truth" posts and one [[fake news]] post during an analysis of party and party leaders' Facebook pages. Krewel attributed the low level of disinformation to the 2026 election's focus on economy and the declining appeal of fringe parties which had capitalised on [[COVID-19]] and vaccine mandates in the previous 2020 and 2023 general elections. In addition, Krewel's study found that several parties including National, Labour and the NZ Outdoors & Freedom Party were using [[TikTok]] and other social media platforms to reach young voters but that target audiences found the posts and memes "cringe" and "inauthentic."<ref>{{cite news |title=TikToks from political parties leave young voters cold - researcher |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/10/25/tiktoks-from-political-parties-leave-young-voters-cold-researcher/ |access-date=28 October 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=25 October 2026}}</ref>

===Strategic voting===
During its campaign launch on 16 August, Te Pāti Māori's co-leadership advocated [[strategic voting]] in order to vote out the National-led coalition government. They urged their supporters to give them their electorate vote while giving their party list vote to either Labour and the Green parties.<ref name="Natanahira 16 Aug 2026" /><ref name="Woodham 29 September 2026">{{cite news |last1=Woodham |first1=Kerre|title=Strategic voting – is it really a clever decision?|url=https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/on-air/kerre-woodham-mornings/opinion/kerre-woodham-strategic-voting-is-it-really-a-clever-decision/ |access-date=1 October 2026 |work=[[Newstalk ZB]] |date=29 September 2026}}</ref>

During the National Party's campaign launch on 27 September, former party leader [[John Key]] and Prime Minister Christopher Luxon sought to dissuade their supporters and voters from splitting their votes in order to ensure that National had a strong representation in Cabinet. Key said that vote splitting would prevent several National MPs from being elected while Luxon described strategic voting as an "insanely stupid idea." In response, ACT and NZ First leaders David Seymour and Winston Peters expressed disagreement and defended strategic voting. Seymour said that both votes belonged to voters and that "it's never wise to talk down to them." Peters accused the National Party of desperation and said that they were "giving out the wrong message" due to efforts by Te Pāti Māori and the left to encourage strategic voting.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT, NZ First hit back at National's warning against strategic voting|url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1631392/act-nz-first-hit-back-at-national-s-warning-against-strategic-voting |access-date=1 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=28 September 2026}}</ref> In addition, businessman [[Nick Mowbray]] launched a website urging right-leaning voters to give their electorate votes to National and their party list votes to either ACT or NZ First to ensure the re-election of a centre-right government.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cooke|first1=Henry|title='An absolute rort': Chris Hipkins hits out at Mowbray overhang campaign |url=https://www.thepost.co.nz/politics/361090539/absolute-rort-chris-hipkins-hits-out-mowbray-overhang-campaign |access-date=1 October 2026 |work=[[The Post (New Zealand newspaper)|The Post]] |date=28 September 2026}}</ref><ref name="Woodham 29 September 2026" />

===Supermarket duopoly===
During the 2026 election campaign, supermarket reform was a key election issue. Two companies, [[Foodstuffs (company)|Foodstuffs]] and [[Woolworths New Zealand]], dominate 82% of [[Supermarkets in New Zealand|New Zealand's supermarket sector]] including retail chains and wholesalers, creating what the [[Commerce Commission]] has described as a "supermarket [[duopoly]]." Their dominance has allowed them to exert pressure on suppliers and charge high grocery prices.<ref name="Baker 22 Sep 2026">{{cite web |last1=Baker |first1=Jonathan |title=Every party has pitched its own supermarkets fix. What if we combined the best ideas? |url=https://theconversation.com/every-party-has-pitched-its-own-supermarket-fix-what-if-we-combined-the-best-ideas-292359 |website=[[The Conversation (website)|The Conversation]] |access-date=2 October 2026 |date=22 September 2026 }}</ref><ref name="The Spinoff supermarkets">{{cite AV media |people=Isaiah Tour and Robbie Nicol |date=22 September 2026 |title=NZ's supermarket duopoly, explained |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/vertical-videos/now-you-know/nzs-supermarket-duopoly-explained |publisher=[[The Spinoff]] |access-date=2 October 2026}}</ref> Five parties have released policies to promote competition in the grocery sector and reduce grocery prices. The National and New Zealand First parties have proposed splitting Foodstuffs into two companies while Labour has proposed forcing the two supermarket companies to split their wholesale and retail divisions. The Greens have proposed forcing Foodstuffs and Woolworths to sell 120 stores and two distribution centres to create a third [[Public grocery store|government-owned supermarket]] chain. While the ACT party has opposed breaking up the two supermarket companies, they have proposed easing planning and regulatory barriers to allow more competitors to enter the New Zealand supermarket sector.<ref name="Baker 22 Sep 2026" /><ref name="The Spinoff supermarkets" /><ref name="RNZ supermarkets">{{cite news |title=Breakups, wholesale splits or public chains? Parties clash over supermarket reform |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1658491/breakups-wholesale-splits-or-public-chains-parties-clash-over-supermarket-reform |access-date=2 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=30 September 2026}}</ref>

In late September 2026, Finance Minister [[Nicola Willis]] said that Foodstuffs and Woolworths were waging a "full-court press" campaign using lobbyists, public relations campaigns, political insiders and influential networks to push back against political efforts to break up the supermarket duopoly.  On 23 September, the pro-business think thank, the [[New Zealand Initiative]], issued an open letter signed by 51 business leaders urging political parties to respect property rights in response to several political parties adopting policies on breaking up the supermarket companies and [[New Zealand electricity market|"gentailers"]], nationalising banks and revising retirement village contracts. The letters' signatories included Foodstuffs' North Island chief executive Chris Quinn and Foodstuff South Island chief executive Mary Devine.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bevin |first1=Andrew |title=Inside the supermarket duopoly's 'full-court press' against political interference |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2026/09/25/inside-the-supermarket-duopolys-full-court-press-against-political-interference/ |access-date=18 October 2026 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=25 September 2026}}</ref>

===Disruptive behaviour and violence===
On 8 September, a group of [[NZ Outdoors & Freedom Party]] anti-vaccination activists including Jeremy Verity and former ACT party marketing director John Ansell confronted Labour MP and candidate [[Ayesha Verrall]] during a candidates event in [[Wadestown, New Zealand|Wadestown]], Wellington. Ansell reportedly accused Verrall of mass murder while livestreaming a social media video. Volunteers and candidates formed a human barrier to protect Verrall. Police were called to the event to manage the situation.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Manera |first1=Ethan |title=Election 2026: Extra security called in to campaign events amid rising threats to politicians |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/wellington/election-2026-extra-security-called-in-to-campaign-events-amid-rising-threats-to-politicians/4FYCCUC5X5EG3D3TGAQXFVTRIM/ |access-date=23 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=10 September 2026}}</ref> Labour leader Hipkins expressed support for Verrall and said that "some people had strong anti-science views and they did not represent the vast bulk of New Zealanders."<ref>{{cite news |title=Labour's Chris Hipkins says his party won't be intimidated by 'anti-science' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1310827/labour-s-chris-hipkins-says-his-party-won-t-be-intimidated-by-anti-science |access-date=23 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=9 September 2026}}</ref> In response to the heckling incident, Verrall issued an open letter calling on election event organisers to moderate proceedings and follow Parliamentary Services' event security guidance. While most [[Wellington North (New Zealand electorate)|Wellington North]] candidates signed the open letter, NZ First candidate Taylor Arneil and Act candidate Matthew Hague declined to sign the letter, dismissing as "performative hypocritical nonsense."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Manera |first1=Ethan |title=Ayesha Verrall's call for political unity over election event disruption leads to further division |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/wellington/ayesha-verralls-call-for-political-unity-over-election-event-disruption-leads-to-further-division/A7UMNDM6PZHEDHTO3MD6Y54WQI/ |access-date=23 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=15 September 2026}}</ref>

On 10 September, left-wing podcaster Mike "Chewie" Bennett, a co-host of the [[Big Hairy Network]], was arrested for refusing to provide police with identifying details after being removed by security from a candidate meeting featuring ACT candidate [[Paul Henry (broadcaster)|Paul Henry]] at [[Dunedin]]'s Glenroy Auditorium. While Bennett accused Henry of violating his free speech, Henry accused Bennett of disrupting the meeting and trespassing. Sixty protesters gathered outside the building while police remained present.<ref>{{cite news |last=Shaw |first=Ruby |title=Podcaster 'frog-marched' from Paul Henry's Dunedin meeting |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/news/dunedin/podcaster-frog-marched-from-paul-henrys-dunedin-meeting-m26q9lb8 |access-date=17 September 2026 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |date=11 September 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Podcast host says he was arrested after challenging Paul Henry at ACT event |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/361031986/podcast-host-says-he-was-arrested-after-challenging-paul-henry-act-event |access-date=17 September 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=11 September 2026}}</ref>

Between 19 and 20 September, ACT's [[Tukituki (New Zealand electorate)|Tukituki]] candidate Rob Douglas was bitten by a dog while campaigning in Havelock North. In response, Douglas and candidates from several other political parties including National, Opportunity, Labour, New Zealand First and the Greens voiced support for a parliamentary review of the Dog Control Act.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hall |first1=Linda |title=Political candidate bitten while campaigning – here's how he'd change dog control laws |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1532008/political-candidate-bitten-while-campaigning-here-s-how-he-d-change-dog-control-laws |access-date=27 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=23 September 2026}}</ref> 

On 26 September, Labour leader [[Chris Hipkins]] was confronted by [[Vision New Zealand]] candidate [[Karl Mokaraka]] while campaigning in Auckland's [[Ōtara]] suburb. Mokaraka followed Hipkins and asked him "What's a woman" before the two were separated by former Labour MP and [[Speaker of the New Zealand House of Representatives|Speaker]] [[Trevor Mallard]].<ref>{{cite news |title='What's a woman?': Vision NZ's Karl Mokaraka heckles Chris Hipkins at Ōtara Markets |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/1589615/what-s-a-woman-vision-nz-s-karl-mokaraka-heckles-chris-hipkins-at-otara-markets |access-date=27 September 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=26 September 2026}}</ref>

On 11 October, several individuals including Dunedin climate activist [[Bruce Mahalski]] and Craig Hinton were denied entry to a New Zealand First event in [[Mosgiel]]'s Coronation Hall featuring party leader Winston Peters by Police and security guards. Mahalski, who had earlier been denied entry to an ACT party event in Dunedin, claimed that police surveillance of activists had "ramped up" and described the police response as an "overkill." In a separate incident, an individual was removed from the event after attempting to ask Peters a question about climate change. An NZ First spokesperson said that one individual had been denied entry for causing disturbances at previous meetings while the other individual had been removed for creating a disturbance during question time.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Scott |first1=Tim |title='it just seems a bit overkill': More people barred from Dunedin election events |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/news/dunedin/it-just-seems-a-bit-overkill-more-people-barred-from-dunedin-election-events-ffv9pudn |access-date=13 October 2026 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |date=12 October 2026|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

Following a [[vehicle ramming attack]] against Australian Member of Parliament [[Helen Haines]] on 19 October, [[Radio New Zealand]] reported that the New Zealand Parliament's "Security Information, Risk and Advisory Service" (SIRAS) handled 216 requests for advice or support between 28 September and 18 October during the election campaign season. During that 21 period, SIRAS was deployed 81 times and assisted MPs attending public events, travelling, and responding to protests and threatening behaviour.<ref name="Hanly threats 20 Oct 2026">{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=Threats against NZ MPs rise 'astronomically', says Speaker |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/2039412/threats-against-nz-mps-rise-astronomically-says-speaker |access-date=28 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=20 October 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Daniels |first1=Chelsea |title=The attack on Helen Haines is a warning New Zealand cannot ignore – The Front Page |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/the-attack-on-helen-haines-is-a-warning-new-zealand-cannot-ignore-the-front-page/2TJQJTFVXRELHCMMQT5TSU7SUA/ |access-date=28 October 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=20 October 2026}}</ref> Parliamentary Service chief executive Rafael Gonzalez-Montero said that MPs had "significant volume of threatening language and abuse" while carrying out their work, with social media serving as an amplifier for abusive behaviour. Speaker of the House [[Gerry Brownlee]] said that increasing threats against MPs in the past three years had forced closer cooperation between Parliament and Police, describing the current arrangement as "the strongest protocol we've ever had with the New Zealand Police."<ref name="Hanly threats 20 Oct 2026" /> 

Similarly, [[1News]] reported that several non-White political candidates including ACT candidate Malkiat Singh and Green Party candidate Tania Waikato had received online racist abuse and death threats in the months leading up to the election. As a result of these threats, Singh was accompanied by volunteers while doing door-to-door campaigning. Waikato described the online messages as a mixture of death threats, racial abuse targeting [[Māori people]] and sexualised slurs. In response to online abuse, 35 candidates including several MPs hired tech firm Areto to screen their social media accounts for abusive comments, with 30% of these comments being "hidden" in the past six months for being too offensive. Areto founder and chief product officer Jacqueline Comer said that racist abuse including [[antisemitism]] and [[Islamophobia]] dominated the offensive content, followed by sexist or gender-based abuse. [[Massey University]] communications professor [[Mohan Dutta]] attributed the rise in [[anti-Indian sentiment|anti-Indian]] and [[anti-Māori sentiment]] online to the growing inequality amidst a cost of living crisis and rising unemployment, and divisive rhetoric by politicians.<ref>{{cite news |title='They’ll make me disappear': Candidates hit by surge in online racism |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/10/26/theyll-make-me-disappear-candidates-hit-by-surge-in-online-racism/ |access-date=28 October 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=26 October 2026}}</ref>

===Vandalism of election hoardings===
During the election season, several election hoardings were targeted by vandals. Vandalism can take the form of gluing googly eyes on candidates' faces, posting offensive comments and symbols, demolishing or burning  hoardings. Vandalism places a strain on the financial resources of candidates, parties and the energy of local volunteers.<ref name="Dirga 28 Sep 2026">{{cite news |last1=Dirga |first1=Nik |title=Explainer: Can you get arrested for vandalising a campaign sign? |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1625437/explainer-can-you-get-arrested-for-vandalising-a-campaign-sign |access-date=3 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=28 September 2026}}</ref><ref name="Shaw 29 Sep 2026">{{cite news |last1=Shaw |first1=Ruby |title=Bashed and beaten billboards no replacement for political debate, candidates say |url=https://www.odt.co.nz/news/dunedin/bashed-and-beaten-billboards-no-replacement-for-political-debate-candidates-say-jm2dk5qg |access-date=3 October 2026 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |date=29 September 2026}}</ref> Under New Zealand law, it is illegal to deface, destroy or remove election hoardings. Vandals can be charged with wilful damage or theft for removing hoardings, with penalties ranging from a three-month prison sentence or a fine of up to $2,000. Depending on the value of the item stolen, penalties for theft range from fines to prison sentences.<ref name="Dirga 28 Sep 2026" /> 

Numerous candidates from different parties across the spectrum have reported that their hoardings have been vandalised including Labour's [[Waikato (electorate)|Waikato]] candidate Jamie Toko, Te Pāti Māori co-leader [[Debbie Ngarewa-Packer]], National MP [[Ryan Hamilton (New Zealand politician)|Ryan Hamilton]], Te Pāti Māori's [[Te Tai Tonga]] candidate Lisa Marie Murch, NZ First [[Taieri (electorate)|Taieri]] list MP [[Mark Patterson (New Zealand politician)|Mark Patterson]], Labour's Taieri MP [[Ingrid Leary]] and National MP [[Carlos Cheung]].<ref name="Dirga 28 Sep 2026" /><ref>{{cite news |title=Election billboards vandalised: Debbie Ngarewa-Packer and Ryan Hamilton hit out |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/election-billboards-vandalised-debbie-ngarewa-packer-and-ryan-hamilton-hit-out/BWGAMBNGD5D2NBYSW6RUIWZEJA/ |access-date=27 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=14 September 2026}}</ref><ref name="Shaw 29 Sep 2026" /><ref name="RNZ Cheung 4 Oct 2026">{{cite news |title=Election signage deliberately set on fire, National MP Carlos Cheung says |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics_election-2026/1712330/election-signage-deliberately-set-on-fire-national-mp-carlos-cheung-says |access-date=13 October 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=4 October 2026}}</ref> On 12 September, police charged a 43-year old man for vandalising 30 election hoardings in [[Pāpāmoa]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Man charged after alleged 'vandalism spree' targeting political billboards |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/nz-news/361032606/man-charged-after-alleged-vandalism-spree-targeting-political-billboards |access-date=17 September 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=12 September 2026}}</ref> On 16 September, police launched an investigation into the overnight burning of a National Party hoarding in Auckland's [[Mount Albert (New Zealand electorate)|Mount Albert electorate]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Election 2026: National hoarding targeted in Mt Albert arson attack, police investigating |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/election-2026-national-hoarding-targeted-in-mt-albert-arson-attack-police-investigating/HJ2IQ3EVRNFYHBKY6IA6L5TWBE/ |access-date=17 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=16 September 2026}}</ref> On 4 October, Cheung reported that his election bollard in [[Mount Roskill]] had been set alight overnight.<ref name="RNZ Cheung 4 Oct 2026" />

